// Build: ASV Simulator - Technical Manual (engineers & programmers)
//
// THE MANUAL IS GENERATED - edit THIS script and rebuild; never hand-edit the docx.
// If the docx is hand-edited in Word anyway: diff that text against the generated
// version (paragraph extraction), fold the edits INTO this script (mark them as the
// author's), then rebuild.
//
//   cd tools && npm install && node build_tech_manual.js
//
// Writes ../docs/asv-simulator-technical-manual.docx (path is script-relative).
//
// BRAND-FREE BY RULE. This console is the generic, vendor-neutral sibling: the onboard
// controller is a VCU, and no vendor or model name appears in the console core or in
// this manual's description of it. Vessel FILES may name real vessels - that is data,
// not branding - so a vessel table quoting a real hull is fine and a "the X console"
// framing is not. Keep it that way when extending this script.
// Shared building blocks. Extracted 2026-08-02 when the document set grew to four
// generators; this manual's output was byte-identical across that extraction - which was
// TRUE and proved nothing, because it was identically broken at the time (see 13.1).
const { P, H1, H2, H3, B, B2, CODE, TBL, SP, TITLE, write } = require("./docx_kit");
const fs = require("fs");
const path = require("path");

// THE HARNESS TABLE IN 13.1 IS DERIVED FROM tests/, not written out by hand. It used to be a
// hand-maintained table introduced by the word "Fourteen", and by the time anyone counted
// there were twenty - the same list-beside-a-directory drift already fixed in the pre-commit
// hook and in the maintainer notes. The DESCRIPTIONS stay hand-written, because "what this
// guards" is prose no parser can invent; the LIST and the COUNT come from the filesystem, and
// a suite nobody has described is called out in the document rather than quietly omitted.
const GUARDS = {
  "coast.js":
    "Coming in on the drift — where the propulsion stops so the vessel arrives at a berth with the way off her, and whether that is safe to attempt. The hull is described by a single coast length, which makes the distance to halve speed the same from any release speed; that invariant is what distinguishes a real coast from the engine-governed ramp, since a constant deceleration would scale the distance as the square of the release speed. The release point is solved backwards in closed form and vectorially, so a set across the approach is handled honestly rather than as an along-track approximation, and the heading is always the ROUTE'S — an earlier design let the solver choose the heading that best stemmed the set, which placed the release point up-set of the berth and, at the operating base that motivated the work, forty meters inside a wharf. The drift track is walked against the same keep-out model the router used, and walked past the berth by the certified clear water, because a hull that carries further than modeled arrives with way still on. Above all the maneuver ends on a SPEED and not on a position: the coast length is an estimate with a third of doubt either way, which over a fifty-meter run is far more than the clear water at a tight berth, so no aim point can be made safe — but ending on a speed means that error moves only where powered control resumes, never how fast she is going when it does.",
  "coast_sim.py":
    "The vessel half of the drift-in: that the way actually comes off under hull drag rather than the engine's ramp, that the coast ENDS, and that a hull with no coast datum does not coast at all. The ending is the one to watch — quadratic drag only asymptotes, so a coast with no speed floor leaves a vessel that stopped short of its berth gliding indefinitely, never arriving, never holding, with the propulsion off; that was a real defect in the first cut of the feature and it was found by driving the maneuver rather than by reading it. Also holds that a fresh plan is never mid-coast, that the propulsion-off state reaches the telemetry (the mission card carried a branch for it long before anything could set it), and that a malformed release range is refused in words rather than becoming a server error.",
  "hold_point.js":
    "Where a boat is asked to hold, and how much water it has there. A hold point needs WORKING MARGIN, not merely to be outside the buffer: the water the set moves the vessel through while the look-ahead ladder is deciding, floored at hull scale, so the number comes from the measured environment rather than from taste — and it grows with the set. A target under that margin is moved to the nearest berth that has it even when it is technically clear, and where the geometry offers a choice the berth is taken down-set of the hazard, so residual drift carries the vessel off the structure and the station-keeping correction is made heading into the set, the direction a boat can stop in. Measured at Eastport on 2026-09-03: HOME had 1.47 m of clear water, was not blocked at the bare buffer, was therefore never even considered for relocation, and the vessel reached 1.74 m from it at 6.07 knots. Watch above all for a check that drives the relocation directly instead of through the gate — that is what let this through, and two mutations survived on it. Also the command-time half of the earlier Eastport work. A commanded route's end is a HOLD POINT, not merely a destination: a target inside the keep-out model is moved to the nearest clear water in ANY direction (a radial search, because the existing one-axis nudge that splits a long leg finds the further water at a pier's end), with the whole hold disc — buffer plus the radius the vessel is allowed to wander — clear, and the move is captured with the plan's reasoning and said on the banner. Only a target with no clear water within the search cap is still refused, and then the refusal names the cap. Every holding command carries the certified clear radius to the vessel, the routed re-approach fires only with authority and only when the vessel asks for it, one at a time with a floor between them, and a refused route back is said rather than left as silence.",
  "hold_station.py":
    "The vessel half of the same work: a station-keeping vessel no longer drives a blind bearing back onto station from any range. It is told, with every holding command, how much water is certified clear round its hold point; inside that disc the direct re-approach is honest (a chord of a clear disc is clear), beyond it the vessel takes the way off and reports that it wants a routed way back, and the engine's re-approach endpoint supplies one that keeps the run's behavior rather than renaming it a Go-To. With no certified disc at all the direct re-approach stays — an honest degrade, the same as a Go-To with no route. Also holds that the hold point and the range to it are on the telemetry from the arrival frame, that the re-approach is refused in words when the vessel is not holding, and that a malformed disc value is a refusal and never a 500. It also holds that an UPLOAD never changes what the boat is doing: a plan uploaded to a running link is STAGED and applied by start() - at any speed commanded after the upload - a staged plan that meets Stop becomes the loaded plan, and over the real engine an upload under way is refused in words while one to a station-keeping boat leaves it on station until Start. It also holds that Stop, E-STOP and a disarm each end station-keeping as well as the run; that a routed re-approach is refused unless the run is under way - after a Stop, while paused, after an E-STOP or a disarm; that a paused station-keeping vessel, resumed, keeps station and keeps its run's name, so an escape stays an escape; that a plan run to its end and started again runs from its first waypoint; and that the vessel makes no way with no leg left and no hold to keep.",
  "escape_chain.py":
    "The in-extremis escape is its own behavior, not a Go-To wearing one — the server half of the fix, since a page check that the client calls the right URL is silent about what the server does once the request lands. Measured live at Eastport, 2026-09-03: the clearance guard steered the boat clear of a pier by commanding a Go-To, the boat held there, and nine seconds later the end-of-plan Return-to-Home chain read that hold as the plan being over and sent it straight back toward the pier — a second escape fired three seconds after that, the safety intervention undoing itself on repeat. /api/cmd/escape now marks the run behavior \"escape\", which the client-side chain and its predictor both refuse to ever read as a finished plan (tests/end_action.js); this suite proves the server actually sets that behavior rather than a leftover \"goto\", that it is gated by the same arm requirement as every other commanded motion, and that a routed re-approach off an escape hold — needed when the same tide that caused the escape sets the boat off its own escape point — keeps it an escape rather than reverting to something the chain no longer recognizes as exempt.",
  "track_edge.js":
    "The look-ahead follows the PLAN, and a near miss is answered by a few meters of track rather than by stopping the boat. Until this work the run-time ladder projected the vessel's present ground velocity forward in a STRAIGHT line for up to forty-five seconds - a maneuver nobody intends to make - while every planner in the console clips or routes to the buffer edge and then turns. A plan is therefore correct precisely when it grazes the buffer, and the guard alarmed forty to sixty meters before that same edge; the two rules could not both be satisfied and the plan lost. Measured against a pier at a five-meter buffer, in dead calm, with no set on the boat at all, on a survey line the punch-out had trimmed exactly as it should: slowed at ninety meters of clearance and STOPPED with thirty-three meters of its own certified water still to run. A false stop beside a structure is not a harmless pause - it takes the way off a hull the tide is already setting, and it destroys the run, because a hold replaces the vessel's plan with a single waypoint and there is no resume. The projection now walks the route from the vessel's actual position and heading, swinging at the hull's own turn rate and adding the drift at every step, so a boat holding its track reads clear because it is, a boat being set off one still reads the entry, and a hull that cannot physically make a corner still reads the entry. With no route to follow - station-keeping, paused, or on the transmitter - it is exactly the projection it always was, which leaves the case that earned the helm rung untouched. Where an entry is predicted and there is open water alongside, the answer is a DEVIATION rather than a stop: the smallest amendment to the track that puts the whole projected path clear, verified by re-projecting it against the same keep-out model at a buffer LARGER than the one that triggered it, so one answer holds instead of chattering. The deviation goes where the trouble is, which on a routed detour is usually a corner two or three waypoints ahead rather than the leg being flown. The console may move a corner; it may never move a DESTINATION. And the operator can override the two rungs that impede the boat - the slow-down and the stop - with one recorded decision that lapses on its own when the episode ends, when the water gets materially worse than the water they looked at, or the moment stopping stops being an answer.",
  "amend_plan.py":
    "The vessel half of the deviation: a running plan can have its unflown remainder replaced without losing the run. Every commanded motion in this console went through the plan upload, which resets the waypoint index to zero - so the only way to change a running plan was to start it again from its first waypoint, and the only intervention cheap enough to make in anger was the throttle. That is why the clearance guard could only slow or stop. The amendment keeps the flown prefix, leaves the index where it is, and keeps the behavior string, for the same reason the routed re-approach does: a Go-To would do the same driving and would rename a survey on every card, re-arm the end-of-plan return, and send the boat back to the beginning. Holds that the new leg is measured from where the vessel actually is rather than from a waypoint that no longer exists - otherwise a deviation that shortens the leg reads as already past the new waypoint and is skipped in the tick it arrives - that an amendment ends a drift-in and does not re-arm it, since a hull with the propulsion off cannot take a deviation at all, and that an amendment is refused, in words rather than as a server error, when there is no running plan to amend, when the vessel is station-keeping, and when the route is empty or malformed.",
  "in_extremis.js":
    "The look-ahead ladder, and the one place the console is permitted to steer. The vessel's ground track is projected forward on every telemetry frame; if it enters the keep-out buffer, the track is projected a SECOND time with the engines notionally stopped, using drift alone. If that drift-only track is clear, taking the way off answers the situation, and the response is to slow and then to hold. If it is not clear, stopping is the one thing that certainly fails — the water is doing the carrying — and only then does the console take the helm, expressed as a dedicated escape behavior the operator can see, stop and override — never a Go-To, whose arrival used to look exactly like an ordinary commanded run and re-chain the end-of-plan Return-to-Home straight back toward whatever it had just steered clear of (measured live at Eastport, 2026-09-03; see tests/end_action.js and tests/escape_chain.py). That distinction is measured rather than argued: a hull lying stopped in a two-knot stream makes two knots over the ground with no force on it at all. Also holds the awkward cases: a position already inside the buffer reports zero seconds rather than no entry; the escape course is scored with the set included rather than through the water, since a heading that is clear through the water and downstream into the pier is not a way out; and a vessel boxed in on every heading is REFUSED an escape and told to take manual control, rather than handed a confident-looking direction that still ends at the pier. It also holds the escape from INSIDE the buffer, where the search used to find nothing - every heading enters at zero seconds - and read BOXED IN with open water behind the boat: a heading there is a way out when its track never comes within half a meter of the feature, leaves the buffer within the look-ahead, and then stays out for a whole look-ahead, however slowly it gets out; the escape point is that whole look-ahead past the edge; a track that crosses a feature, or leaves one buffer only to enter another, is refused; and ties between clear headings go to the clearance kept along the whole track, then at its end - never to distance over the ground, which with the set running along a face chose the heading along the face.",
  "target_cpa.js":
    "CPA and TCPA for every AIS contact - the closest the two vessels will come on present course and speed, and how long until that happens - plus the hull footprint a contact broadcasts. Both are pure functions of the current kinematics, so they follow a maneuver by either vessel without any cached value to go stale or any maneuver detector to miss one. Holds the awkward cases too: a contact already drawing away reports a NEGATIVE time rather than having its past closest approach shown as though it were ahead, two vessels holding station get a range but no time rather than an enormous one divided out of report jitter, and a contact broadcasting no track gets nothing rather than a zero that would assert it is stopped. The hull is anchored on the GNSS antenna the position actually comes from, not centered, which on a large ship with the bridge aft is most of the vessel s length.",
  "ais_particulars.py":
    "The vessel particulars AIS already broadcasts and the service used to discard: hull dimensions, ship type, destination, draught, IMO number and call sign, decoded from message 5, message 19 and message 24 part B at their own distinct bit offsets and verified against a published conformance sentence. Also that the not-available encodings are honoured - all-zero dimensions are an absent hull rather than a zero-meter one, and the text padding is an absent destination rather than an empty one - that static particulars survive the position reports that follow them, and that every one of them reaches the served record rather than being dropped at the serialiser, which builds its output from an explicit list of keys.",
  "direct_turn.js":
    "The wharf ladder is still there, and its window moved when the test became one about a boat rather than about a polyline: with the water squeezed to where a hull needs more room than it has, the ladder must refuse rather than ship a loop the boat would clip - which is only safe because a refused reversal has been flagged unsafe and blocked at upload since it stopped shipping as a straight leg. Swept across the fixture the flyability test costs nothing in ordinary water and bites only in the last half meter. The DIRECT (racetrack) reversal and its place in the turn ladder. A survey turn used to be an arc of HALF THE LINE OFFSET whenever the hull could hold it — 15.75 m on a Z-Boat at 31.5 m line spacing, against a hull radius of 2.06 m — and such an arc needs its full radius of clear water past the end of the line. Where a wharf took that water away, the only rung left swept the same arc the other way: back across 33 m of just-surveyed water. The racetrack asks instead for the radius the boat actually has, as two quarter-circles joined by a straight run: 32% shorter, and reaching the hull radius past the line end at ANY spacing, so a turn that previously had to invert now stays outboard and away from the feature. Also holds the rest of the ladder in place — that the gentle arc remains the first thing tried for every unobstructed turn, that the inboard rungs remain available below (they are the rung the wharf incident bought, and refusing a turn is what put that boat on the pier), and that the arc is stepped finely enough for the vessel to arrive on the heading of the next line.",
  "strike_run.js":
    "Striking a punched survey run off by hand and rebuilding the inter-line transits around the gap: that a strike is remembered as a MIDPOINT rather than an index (so it names the run itself, survives the turn-margin shortening and the region ordering, and stops naming anything rather than naming the wrong thing when the pattern moves); that the filter sits after the chart clip and before the ordering, which is the whole of what got the equivalent build reverted in the sibling application; that the rebuild is coalesced onto one punch and that anything reading the plan as a result waits for it, since committing inside the window would commit the plan from before the strike; that a reversal across the resulting gap is still built and still reported, rather than falling through to a straight 180 no hull can track; and that the selection highlight is wide enough to see, which is a check that exists because the first one measurably was not",
  "enc_roles.js":
    "That every ENC class the server requests has ONE decided meaning in the keep-out model: obstruction LINES arrive as paths, bridge SUPPORTS as rings, cardinal buoys as hazards rather than lateral marks, and the bridge SPAN — which is overhead — as nothing at all, since enforcing it would refuse passage under every bridge on the chart. Also that `extra`, the whole rest of the published chart now downloaded on entering an area, reaches the model as nothing; that no class is requested under two roles; and that no role is fetched without a decided meaning. Check 8 asks it the durable way — every geometry form the server requests must arrive somewhere — after the first version, which counted output buckets, failed to catch the very bug it was written for",
  "off_track.js":
    "That 'off track' on the Intent card is the SIGNED perpendicular from the leg being flown rather than the range to the nearest survey line: that it names its own subject, that it can distinguish left from right, and that a boat past the end of a leg is measured against the leg rather than snapped to its endpoint",
  "port_slew.js":
    "The port change as a visible MOVE rather than a teleport: that the chart flies base-to-base at constant screen speed over integer tile zooms, that BOAT-follow stays off for the whole flight (the respawned sim boat's telemetry beats the fetch reply), and that the leg is drawn on the canvas because the tiles under it are not yet fetched",
  "clearance_guard.js":
    "A SAFETY SUITE, and since 2026-09-10 the place the ladder's ASYMMETRY is pinned: it slows on the frame it sees the trouble and hands the throttle back only after the water has read clear for a dwell, because one frame's opinion put thirty-six speed commands into thirty-five seconds on a real run. The dwell is driven over a synthetic minute rather than a real one - a dwell tested by sleeping is a dwell nobody runs - and the checks hold three things apart: that a single clear frame does not release, that an alternating reading never releases at all, and that one bad frame restarts the dwell rather than shortening it, which a version counting total clear frames would fail on exactly the pattern it exists for. The dwell is also asked once a frame ABOVE the branch, so no path can skip the reset - written as a reset on the not-clear path it survived a mutation sweep, being a line anybody could delete with nothing reddening. Beside it sits the rung that answers a TURN: a deviation cannot, since the console may move a corner and a reversal is a run of vertices a meter apart, but a turn's trouble is tracking and the lever on tracking is speed - so before the ladder takes the way off it re-asks the projection at the low speed and slows instead of stopping when that clears it, driven on the same pier fixture the in-extremis suite uses, with both refusals pinned: not when she is already slow, because then low is the state being assessed, and not when the drift-only track enters too, because that is the helm's case and stopping is no answer to it either.  written after a DriX passed a wharf at 0.6 m with a 5 m buffer set. The turn LADDER — a refused reversal is retried outboard, then inboard, then at the slow radius, and refused as UNSAFE rather than shipped as a straight 180 the hull cannot track — and the RUNTIME guard: live clearance built from the keep-out model's own primitives, alarming inside the buffer and commanding low speed when closing, never a heading, and never anything at all unless the boat is under autonomous command It also holds that a slow-down offered IN LIEU of a hold has to be TAKEN and keep answering: driven over consecutive frames, a speed command that never lands (the vessel keeps reporting survey, or on a link with no key the speed over ground does not drop) is followed by the hold within SLOW_ANSWER_MS, and so is a boat that slowing no longer answers from where it now is - while a hull genuinely coming down is left to do it. It also holds that the slow rung reads the speed the vessel REPORTS, not the configured role speed. It also holds that a refused escape says which refusal it was: from outside the buffer, every heading enters a keep-out; from inside it, no heading gets out of the buffer and stays out.",
  "speed_modes.js":
    "Speed by ROLE: that a survey run's three jobs — coverage, turns, transits — each carry their own operator-selected speed, that the governor commands the live one without ever overwriting the operator's setting, that the clearance guard outranks it, and that the turn RADIUS is built from the TURN speed because a hull holds v/omega It also holds that every speed the console commands is reconciled against the speed_key the vessel reports: re-sent after SPEED_RESEND_MS of disagreement and not before, never re-sent once taken, dropped when the boat is not under command, and told to the operator once after SPEED_RESEND_MAX tries.",
  "rule9_scope.js":
    "WHERE COLREGS Rule 9 applies, which is only within a narrow channel or fairway and only on a transit: that a charted FAIRWY or DRGARE puts the rule in force, that water genuinely under the width limit does too, that 300 m of open bay does NOT, that survey patterns are never laned, and that a fairway is not quietly made a keep-out — which would refuse every route down the middle of a marked channel",
  "buoy_lane.js":
    "The Rule 9 channel lane: which side of a channel the vessel rides, marked and unmarked, both directions, that a lone buoy is not treated as a wall, that the lane fact travels WITH the route it describes, WHERE THE LANE LETS GO — still at full offset at the final buoy pair, still standing on one channel width past it, held to the end of a charted channel that outruns the buoyage, released rather than carried across the gap to a separate channel, reported as PARTIAL when a stretch went un-laned, a per-vessel reach override that can only widen the wall search — and that NO SHIPPED ROUTE carries a reversal knot tighter than a hull can fly: the knot prune runs inside the lane producer on the clearance gate's own output, so a splice seam cannot fold a waypoint the vessel must orbit to capture",
  "turn_geometry.js":
    "Survey turns, and whether the HULL can fly the shape as well as whether the DRAWING clears: every candidate on the ladder is projected exactly as the runtime clearance guard will project it - same integrator, same turn rate, same approach radius, same keep-out model - so a shape the boat would fly through a feature is refused at plan time instead of stopping the survey at run time. The two used to answer different questions about the same turn. Under that sits a floor on waypoint SPACING, which is the part nobody would guess: the vessel and the guard both advance to the next waypoint the moment they are within the approach radius of it, so a run of vertices a fifth of a metre apart is swallowed in a single step and the boat is left steering at a point half way round the loop, flying a chord across the inside of its own turn. Measured on a real plan, the same reversal drawn three and three quarter meters off a keep-out either way put the flown track one and a half meters from it when sampled finely and four meters from it when sampled at the approach radius - sampling a curve finer than the thing that follows it makes the boat fly it worse. So the shape is thinned to that floor BEFORE it is verified, because checking the dense shape and shipping the thinned one is the same fault one layer down; the flyability horizon is the shape's own length rather than the guard's forty-five seconds, or a long turn stops being checked half way round; the projection runs at the hull's own turn rate; the check is armed unless a caller disables it by name, so forgetting it cannot silently disarm a safety test; and an eased turn whose settle length cannot ramp across the spacing is not offered, because what would ship is an arc wearing the word eased. Also: shape selection, the minimum radius held along the WHOLE path, exit alignment, outboard excursion, and nogo refusal paired with its clear-water twin. Above all, that a reversal is built from the two line ends' HEADINGS and not from the chord between them. An arc drawn on that chord has its tangents perpendicular to the chord rather than to the lines, which is the same shape only while the two ends are abeam; give the pair any along-track offset and the vessel is thrown off the line it has just run and arrives on the next one crabbing, by exactly the angle the offset subtends. Lead-in and lead-out extensions open such an offset at every reversal, and the chart clip opens one on its own, so the answer is to run the offset out ON the line before arcing - exactly as the racetrack and the teardrop have always done - and to take both the radius and the branch decision from the CROSSING rather than the chord: gated on the chord, a tight crossing with a long offset hands the vessel a radius below the one it can physically hold. And the reach past the line end is measured off the points in every shape rather than asserted as the radius, because with a run-out the shape reaches the offset plus the radius, and that reach is the figure the operator answers 'is that water clear?' with. The suite also holds the EASED reversal - a clothoid, a circular core, and a clothoid out - which exists because every other shape here steps its curvature from nothing to the reciprocal of its radius the instant the vessel leaves the line, an infinite rudder rate that the hull answers by overshooting and settling. The eased shape ramps instead, so the steering rate is constant and the vessel rolls onto the next line with the helm already amidships. Its radius is SOLVED against the integrated crossing rather than taken from the closed form that seeds it - established by mutation, since a deliberately wrong seed still converges - and the sampling is part of the shape rather than a detail: a polyline cannot express curvature continuity, so a ramp emitted at the ordinary arc step is an arc wearing the word eased, and the spirals are emitted eight chords apiece while the constant-curvature core keeps the ordinary step. Measured on a live seven-line survey, the reversals that took the eased shape were about seven times gentler at their worst waypoint for roughly half again as many waypoints. It is a rung on top of the turn ladder and never a replacement, so where the eased shape will not fit the plan gets precisely the turn it would have had otherwise, and the spiral length comes from the vessel file - how long that hull's steering takes to reach the commanded rate - so a profile that has never been asked the question cannot ease at all.",
  "roc_tracks.py":
    "ROC arrival geometry, the staged/active HOME gate, moving HOME, every vessel-derived default, and NMEA ingest robustness - a malformed sentence must cost one sentence, not the whole GPS link",
  "completion_modes.py":
    "The operator's end-of-plan SETTING versus the completion of the run in progress: that a behavior can never change the setting, and that a plan run adopts it. Drives a real console over the API",
  "water_trust.js":
    "That a live water level from a distant tide station is flagged as such rather than shown as if it were local, and that the nearest contributing station decides the banding. And (review #13) that a level past WATER_STALE_S is not applied, at any distance, while one at the limit is; that a manual override is never stale; and that a missing or non-numeric age is unknown, not old",
  "reading_age.js":
    "That every reading on the card says how old it is (review #13), driving the page's own updateWaterUI, updateEnvUI and updateCurrentUI: the water row and the survey panel's note show the age, and a stale level is ghosted, marked (datum) and explained in its tooltip - with both reasons when it is remote as well; the wind row shows its oldest buoy report's age, and none under a manual override or with the simulator's weather off; the current shows an age only past five minutes; and a failed update is named in each tooltip",
  "reading_age.py":
    "The server half of review #13: that the water level, weather and current monitors survive a pass that raises - the error is carried on the snapshot, printed once, the last reading kept and the error cleared by the next good pass - that a connection cut mid-body is a failed fetch in words and a damaged station cache is fetched again; that the water level is aged from its own observation time and the weather from its oldest buoy; that a manual override or a failed reading has no age; and that the current is recomputed every SAMPLE_S, looks for its cycle every POLL_S or at once when forced, and says why when there is no cycle",
  "wreck_clearance.js":
    "Charted point-hazard extent, and whether the hazard belongs in the model at all. A wreck symbol is a POSITION: the casualty under it can be a hundred-meter ship and the chart says nothing about its extent or which way it lies, so a wreck, hulk, obstruction or awash rock carries an assumed radius and the buffer is added on top as the margin it was always meant to be - the suite holds that in BOTH the exact leg check and the search raster, because a route the leg check refuses but the raster plans through is a refusal rather than a detour. Genuinely point-sized objects - piles, buoys, beacons, mooring points - keep the plain buffer, and no sounding charted on one ever changes that, because they are obstructions at the surface. Where the chart DOES carry a sounding over a hazard whose extent it does not give, and that sounding clears the floor in force by the margin once corrected for the live water level, the hazard is dropped from the keep-out model outright rather than merely losing its assumed extent. Zeroing the extent was not enough and the difference is not academic: a point in this model still carries the operator's buffer, so a rock with nine meters of water over it stayed a small no-go disc, and a disc sitting a fraction of a meter off a survey line cut that line in two - leaving an eight-meter offcut that the punch then serviced with a full pair of reversals, spending seventy-seven meters of track and sixteen waypoints to collect under eight meters of coverage. Every direction of that rule is paired with its control: no charted sounding is unknown and unknown keeps the full berth; a sounding that does not clear the floor by the margin is not a pass; the correction is the live water level, so a hazard marginal at datum returns to the model on a falling tide; and the floor the rule is judged at is the EFFECTIVE one - the deeper of the hull's own limit and the operator's minimum depth - never the hull's alone, because a test that REMOVES a keep-out must not be decided at a shallower floor than the water around it. A dropped hazard is still drawn on the chart, and the model reports how many it dropped and why, because a model that leaves something out has to be able to account for it. Also that the vessel's own buffer is a floor a stale mission cannot undercut, and that the assumed radius is vessel-configurable",
  "end_action.js":
    "That the cards name where the run leaves the vessel: an end-of-plan return-to-home shows from the START of a Go-To, transit or survey; is not promised when the chain cannot fire; and re-arms for a run commanded while already under way. Also the INTENT card: that a plan's reasoning is captured WHEN IT IS COMMITTED and never re-derived, that routed and direct plans are told apart, that a partial Rule 9 lane reads differently from a full one, that legs with no clear detour are counted as unsafe, that a plan built with no keep-out model warns instead of reporting a clear run, that waypoint roles are labeled only from what is known, and that every place the route is cleared drops its reasoning with it. And that SURVEYING is distinguished from TRANSITING: only a vessel on a coverage line is surveying, while the approach, the reversals between lines, region hops, go-tos and returns home are transits - one classifier answering for the cards and for the session log, so continuously-collected data can be segmented later without a second implementation free to disagree. And the vessel marker itself: that it is an isosceles triangle whose apex leads along the line of travel, rotated by the course in radians, that a two-tone livery fills the aft band without ever covering the pointed end, and that the hull color is read from the vessel profile rather than a table of ids in the page",
  "nogo_readout.js":
    "The keep-out readout's state: that it stops reporting reading once the extract has landed, and that clear water and no chart at all - both zero keep-outs - never read as the same thing",
  "pattern_move_grip.js":
    "That the survey pattern's whole-move grip is both REACHABLE by hit test and VISIBLE - drawn above the vessel marker that used to cover it completely",
  "survey_card.js":
    "That a committed survey plan is still described rather than blanking when its pattern anchors are dropped, and that the figures are DERIVED from the committed lines and self-validating on parallelism",
  "frame_health.js":
    "The page half of review #12: that the safety loop does not fail without a sign. It drives the page's own onFrame, consoleHealth and connect with the clock, the banner and fetch stubbed, evaluated strict as the module runs: a failed frame never escapes; three say CONSOLE FAULT naming the error and are logged once; the fault stays up through seven clean frames and comes down on the eighth in a row, and a fault on every other frame is a fault, not a flicker; a message that is not a state frame is no fault and no update; no frame for over 2 s while the console says it streams says TELEMETRY STALE with the dot red and the pill stale - with the link lost too - but not with no link or before the first frame; the console's own loop fault is said, after the page's when both hold, and neither is said while stale; and a banner that is not its own is never taken down.",
  "drawn_lines.js":
    "That a line is what the operator drew, not what the keep-outs left of it (review #18): a line Punch Out cut into two parts is one line with one number, and the numbering follows plan edits; a part must lie on the same straight line, further along it, in the same direction - the next pass beside it, the line run back, or a segment starting inside the last one is not; the LINES table has one row per drawn line, reading its parts and the gap, summing its plan and actual, under way while either part is, with the RTH row leaving from the last drawn line; the session log keeps the segment as line and adds the drawn one; the survey card counts drawn lines and their true spacing; the activity names the part; and the chart labels, turn table, line tip, held-survey bar and resume notes all use the numbering, which nothing in the page shadows",
  "post_json.py":
    "That a POST must say it is JSON and a stop is never refused (review #25), on the console's own handler over real HTTP with a sim link: an Arm labeled text/plain, form-urlencoded, multipart or not labeled at all is refused 415 in words and the boat stays disarmed; a plan POSTed as text/plain never reaches the plan file; JSON is obeyed with a charset or capitals; Pause and Stop are obeyed whatever they are labeled, a garbled body included; an E-STOP latch is obeyed unlabeled while a release needs JSON; an E-STOP whose body cannot be read or does not say off latches, and only false or 0 releases; every refusal is logged with its reason; the browser's preflight is not granted and no response offers cross-origin access; and every POST the console's own pages make is labeled JSON",
  "action_history.js":
    "That the Mission Status card keeps a history of what the console did (review #22), driving the page's own recorder, row builder, renderer, cmd, cmdLabel, flashNote and showBanner over a DOM stub that counts inserts, removes and markup writes: an answered command is one line with the operator's word for it and the server's note in reply, and a reply that leaves the note unchanged borrows no earlier answer; a refused or dropped command is one line with the refusal in words, and the refusal still flashes; a flashed note is kept; a banner is kept when posted, not when re-asserted on screen, and posted again it moves its line up counted, matched with its numbers set aside; commands keep their order; a repeat of the newest counts up; twenty are kept, newest first; the list survives a reload and damaged storage gives back only whole entries without throwing; rows are patched - a repeat rewrites its row, a new entry inserts one row and drops the last - and a message is text, never markup; a long message is cut with its full text in the tooltip; commands are named in the operator's words; and the section is on the card, declared and drawn before any code that can post a banner",
  "preview_ink.js":
    "That a preview never looks like the route (review #19), driving the page's own drawPattern and drawSearch on a canvas that records every stroke's ink: a punched survey preview's kept lines are cyan, solid over the faint raw lines, with or without a lead, and never the uploaded route's green; a selected run keeps its amber halo with cyan inside; the preview is labeled PREVIEW - not in plan, in cyan with a dark halo, above its top-left; a clipped search preview's kept legs are cyan with its detours amber and its blocked legs red, and it is labeled below its datum; the uploaded route is the only green stroke in the page and the committed lines stay yellow; and the help text says cyan",
  "line_table_patch.js":
    "That the LINES table is patched in place, not rebuilt on every telemetry frame (review #20), driving the page's own renderLineTable over a DOM that counts every write and reads a color back the way a browser does: frames with nothing changed write nothing and look nothing up; a changed actual time edits that cell's own text node and the total; the run highlight and the line-under-way mark move once, not once a frame; a survey speed change re-times the plan cells and leaves the transit rows alone, and a transit speed change re-renders only those two rows; a new turn or a plan edit (a lead added without changing the coverage length) builds the body once; an empty plan writes its note once; a closed panel writes nothing; and every cell the patcher writes exists in the body it built",
  "precommit_hook.py":
    "That the commit gate has no gaps (review #17), by running a copy of the real hook with a stand-in git: the suites run for a commit touching only the current model, a vessel file, the default ports, a module or a tool - paths the old list never named - and for one that stages nothing, and are skipped only when everything staged is Markdown or git metadata; a failing suite blocks the commit with its advice, and one that hangs is stopped after the time limit as TIMED OUT; every suite has exactly one advice entry; and the shared console-output check counts tracebacks, socketserver's handler report and routes that raised, never a deliberate note that names an error",
  "build_check.js":
    "The page half of review #15: that the page says when it and the console are different versions, driving the page's own buildCheck with PAGE_BUILD filled in the way the console fills it. Nothing before the first frame or while they agree; RESTART CONSOLE, naming both versions, when the program on disk is not the one running, and when the console reports no version at all; RELOAD PAGE when the console runs a version the page was not loaded from - with the restart said first when both hold; nothing for a page nobody filled in; the pill hidden again, not left up empty; and onFrame checking every frame",
  "build_id.py":
    "The server half of review #15, on a temp copy of the program: that the state carries the version the console started from and the one on disk - equal at start - and the page is served with it; that rewriting line endings or touching a file is not a new version; that a change to a module the page loads, or to the Python, is a new version on disk within BUILD_RECHECK_S while the console still reports the one it started from, and the page served from then on carries the new one; that a console started again runs the new version; and that the fingerprint is the files, not their folder",
  "plan_save.js":
    "The page half of the plan revision (review #10). saveMission debounces into flushMission, which sends the revision the page holds, adopts the one the console answers, and sends one save at a time. A save refused as made to an older revision is said plainly - edits on this page are not being kept, reload - and the page stops saving; any other failed save is said and tried again with the next change; and loading the plan takes its revision. Driven with fetch stubbed and every answer held open, and a wait that never ends is reported as a failure rather than a silent exit. It also holds (review #11) that a page which has not loaded the plan saves nothing - a failed load, an error body or an answer that is not a plan leaves it not loaded, says PLAN NOT LOADED and why, and retries in 3 s, while an empty plan IS a plan - and that RESET, CLR PLAN and dropping a held survey's remainder ask with guiConfirm's always option even in the simulator: a no destroys nothing, a yes does what it says.",
  "pause_resume.js":
    "That a paused survey is resumed in a way that closes the hole the pause opened. A pause is not a freeze: the propulsion stops, the vessel is set by wind and stream while it waits, and the coverage stops with it - so picking the plan up from wherever she drifted to would butt two runs of coverage together at an angle with a gap between them. The resume instead rejoins the line TWELVE BOAT LENGTHS BEHIND the point the operator pressed the button, over water already surveyed, so the new coverage overlaps the old before it reaches the hole; that is the lead-in's argument answered at a different moment, and the distance is expressed in hulls rather than meters because what a vessel needs to settle and to lay down overlap scales with the vessel. The mark is taken at the PRESS and never re-derived, since re-reading the position on resume would return the drift instead. The direction to back up in is read off the route rather than assumed, because backing the wrong way would drive her into water never surveyed and lay the overlap on the far side of the gap. Backing past the start of the run is clamped to the run, since the water beyond it is the turn she has just flown. The remainder of the plan is AMENDED rather than re-uploaded - an upload resets the waypoint index and would re-run the survey from its first line - and the amendment is made while still paused, before the run is released, so she never makes way under the old plan. A way back that is not clear gives up the backtrack and not the resume. And the run continues at low speed until the operator changes it, with the governor standing down meanwhile so it cannot re-assert the role speed in the act of carrying the instruction out. It also drives the Upload path: with no position fix Upload refuses in words; with the chart model not loaded it ASKS through guiConfirm's `always` option, which shows the dialog even in the simulator, sends nothing on a no, and on a yes sends the plan unrouted with a banner that stays up; a loaded model routes as before with no question. It also holds that each step of the resume lands before the next goes - a refused LOW does not start the run - and that cmd() answers a network error or a refusal with ok:false. It also holds that Upload checks the published waypoint limit before it draws or sends anything: a plan, or its routed path, over the limit is blocked in words with both numbers, and one at the limit goes as before.",
  "guard_resume.js":
    "That the clearance guard stopping a survey is not the end of the survey - and that the record of it survives the two moments it was being thrown away. A hold command uploads its one-waypoint plan and STARTS it, so the vessel reports station-keeping only once it has ARRIVED: five seconds, live, in which the behavior is already the hold and the holding flag is still false. An offer that demanded the flag spent the captured survey on the very next telemetry frame. And the hold rung fires more than once as a clearance closes - three times in five seconds on the run that found this - so a capture that cleared its own record before checking whether it had anything to replace it with threw away what the first firing had saved. Neither of those was in the fixture the first time round, because it set the behavior and the holding flag in the same breath and never called the capture twice. The guard's HOLD rung uploads a one-waypoint plan over the running plan, so the moment it lands the vessel's plan IS the hold point and the unflown remainder exists nowhere but the console - which is why the remainder is captured in the frame BEFORE the hold command goes out, at the waypoint index the survey was at rather than the one the hold leaves behind. And once the vessel is station-keeping the ladder reads clear by construction, because the hold rung is only ever reached when the drift-only track is clear and taking the way off is what produces that state - so the box that named the hazard used to go out within a frame of the survey being stopped, leaving nothing on screen to say the run was over. The offer outlives the rung that caused it, names what the vessel was stopped off and how many waypoints are unflown, and stands only while the vessel is still holding under the guard's own hold. Resuming re-uploads the remainder and starts it, PAUSING FIRST: an upload clears the holding state and adopts the transit role's speed while the run is still live, so an upload to a station-keeping vessel releases it at the fastest speed on the card before the start that was meant to release it is ever sent. Everything else is the pause resume deliberately - twelve boat lengths back down the line, the way back checked against the chart, the backtrack and not the resume given up when that water is foul, and low speed until the operator changes it. The forcing itself is one record with two strengths: a full PROCEED suppresses both rungs that impede the vessel, while CONTINUE AT LOW suppresses only the one that STOPS it, because keep surveying slowly is not keep surveying at survey speed. Neither suppresses the alarm, the clearance readout, a deviation, or the helm in extremis, and both lapse the same three ways. The low speed is the operator's and not the guard's, so the safety ladder's own release cannot restore the survey speed behind them, and both the decision and what it did are announced and written to the session log - an overridden safety intervention that the recording does not mention cannot be read afterwards. It also holds that a held-survey resume whose LOW is refused does not start: the boat is put back on station and the offer stays up.",
  "survey_lead.js":
    "The lead-in and lead-out extensions, and the seam they open: from the moment a survey line is extended past its coverage, the water the boat RUNS and the water it SURVEYS are two different lengths, and every figure on every card has to say which one it means. A lead is flown water, so it is clipped against the chart sample by sample like any other flown water - the clipped run ends either where the operator's box ran out or where the chart said stop, nothing can tell those apart from the endpoint alone, and an extension nobody checked is an extension into a pier; where it will not fit, the card says how much was bought against how much was asked for. It is applied AFTER the hull's minimum-survey-line filter, because a forty-meter run with a thirty-meter lead at each end is a forty-meter SURVEY line and must still be dropped for the reason it always was; AFTER the strike-off filter, because a struck run is remembered by its coverage midpoint and an uneven lead moves the run's own midpoint seventeen meters off it; and BEFORE the turns, because the turn happening past the coverage is the entire point of the feature. A lead is NOT coverage - the line lengths, the card's line length and the surveying flag all still mean coverage - but it IS the survey speed, since settling the boat at any other speed settles it at the wrong one, so the speed role stays survey while the surveying flag goes false. Distance or duration, the operator's choice; the stored value is the one they typed in the unit they chose, because storing the converted meters would freeze a settling TIME into a distance and the next speed change would quietly leave a lead that settles nothing. And the lead GIVES WAY to the turn: a lead pushes the reversal outboard, so it can take a turn that fitted and make it not fit, and rather than block the upload over a settling distance the punch shortens both leads on that pair and retries the whole turn ladder at each step. The last rung is no lead at all, which is the pair the console punched before the feature existed, so a lead can never be the reason a plan carries an unflyable turn. Scaling both is the right lever because the water a reversal needs past the coverage end is the LARGER lead plus the turn radius.",
  "currents.py":
    "That the surface-current readout at the vessel's position is wired honestly: the monitor answers rather than raising or blocking, every way a reading can fail to be live is said in words (no cycle cached, no model water at the position, a value projected by tidal cycles, or a refusal past the projection cap), a projected value is flagged as an estimate while a live frame is not, the reading is fed the vessel's own fix and travels on the state, and it is NOT gated on simulator mode - the simulator invents the wind, but nobody invents the tide",
  "speed_recalc.js":
    "That the plan speed is an INPUT: changing it recalculates turn geometry, durations and per-line times, and a plan already committed is re-checked against the radius the new speed implies. Also the Lines card's transit and RTH rows: ENC-ROUTED distances (boat to line 1, last line home), each row degrading alone when its input is missing, a planner refusal surfaced in the planner's own words, times derived from the current plan speed with no re-route, and a cache key that ignores boat jitter but re-keys on real motion",
  "live_speed.py":
    "That a commanded speed change REACHES the vessel rather than only the readout: under way, speed over ground follows the command in both directions. Drives a real console, because accepting the command proves nothing It also holds that the live speed is the VESSEL'S and not the plan file's: a speed change carries into the next commanded motion and is not written into mission.json.",
  "ais_range.py":
    "That the traffic range filters a wider collected subscription as a true range circle, is clamped to what was collected, is never applied on an enclosed lake, reads in nautical miles, and that an empty card distinguishes nothing-tracked from nothing-in-range",
  "ui_split.js":
    "That every selector the two-window bridge names still resolves, that the vessel-status card renders on the chart window only, and that a card cannot grow past the screen in either direction",
  "stored_settings.js":
    "That every stored operator setting goes through ONE guarded pair of storage helpers, so a browser with site data blocked cannot take out start-up, and that toggles saved by an older build still read",
  "panel_drag.js":
    "That ONE drag mechanism serves every floating pop-out, that none forgets where it was put, and that a restored position is CLAMPED into the chart - a position saved on a larger window used to put a card, and its own reopen control, off the screen entirely. Also the chart's wind rose, the one movable thing that is deliberately NOT a panel: that it claims a press before any editing mode can act underneath it, that it persists where it was dropped, and that its position is clamped on every read rather than on save",
  "ais_table.js":
    "That the traffic list is PATCHED in place rather than rebuilt each poll, so an update cannot reset the card's scroll position while it is being read, nor drop a text selection part way through a copy. And the contact markers themselves: that the operator's ship-type colors are what is drawn, that no two categories share a color, that a dark hull is given a light outline so a black tug is not lost against a near-black chart, and that contacts are drawn with the SAME glyph as the vessel under command rather than a shape of their own",
  "http_contract.py":
    "The two OPPOSITE handler contracts - a POST handler RETURNS (code, obj) while a GET handler commits its own response - across both servers, plus the check no client-side assertion can make: that the server logged no exception while serving them",
  "tide_note.py":
    "That the tide card reports ONE cause ONCE: two failed requests to the same upstream must not print the same failure twice, and must still say BOTH series were lost rather than only the observed one",
  "docs_valid.py":
    "That the generated documents are valid packages a reader will actually open, and that their code blocks reached the page - validity alone would not notice content silently dropped",
  "estop_chain.py":
    "That E-STOP actually reaches the VESSEL and is not merely believed by the console: the commanded latch and the link's own reported state are asserted SEPARATELY, because cutting the seam leaves every console flag looking correct. Also that it latches, refuses commands while latched, and releases cleanly; and (review #27) that a latch the link REFUSES still latches the console - disarmed, idle, saying the vessel did not take it - while a release the link refuses leaves it latched",
  "ais_restart.py":
    "That an AIS service the console started is started again when it exits (review #27), driving the console's own watchdog on an explicit clock over fake processes: the exit is seen on the next look and the service started once the wait has passed; a service that dies at start waits twice as long each time up to the ceiling; one that stayed up goes back to the shortest wait; a service the console stopped, or one it found already on the port, is never restarted; a restart that produced nothing is tried again; the exit is printed and logged; and the console's own start spawns ONE daemon watchdog that notices a death by itself and outlives a look that raises",
  "cache_writes.py":
    "That a cache file is written whole or not at all by any number of writers (review #27): two threads writing one cache with a third reading it never produce a torn or missing file or a failure but a refused replace, and leave no temp file; an interrupted write keeps the old cache byte for byte; the NDBC and CO-OPS station lists are written through a temp of their own and a replace; a cache write the disk refuses costs only the cache, never the stations just fetched; and every named cache writer goes through _cache_json and nothing else",
  "page_strict.js":
    "That the page and its modules parse as the browser parses them - as STRICT modules (review #27): the console page is one module script and parses; every module under static/js parses; the control shows each strict-only fault a sloppy eval accepts is refused here; and no suite still says the console's script runs sloppy",
  "storage_watch.py":
    "That the console says what it keeps on disk and removes nothing (review #24), driving its own walk and StorageWatch on temp folders: the walk counts what logs/ and charts/ hold and what they occupy on the drive's allocation units, with the log older than thirty days split off; a folder it cannot read is skipped; a measurement publishes both folders, the old log and the room left on the drive the plan, the session log and the chart cache write to, and changes no file; under 2 GB free it is low and the report says what writes there and that nothing is removed automatically; allocation units that double a folder's size are explained in the report; the watch reports at start and then only on a change, and logs it; the state carries the measurement and main() starts the watch, not the import; a link or junction inside a folder is not walked, a folder that is a link is measured and priced where it leads, and a drive is named by where its files are; and nothing in the watch can remove, truncate or write a file",
  "storage_banner.js":
    "The page half of review #24, driving the page's own storageCheck with the banner slot and the pill stubbed: nothing is said while there is room; a drive gone low raises one banner in twenty frames, naming the room left, the drive, what writes there, what logs/ and charts/ occupy and that nothing is removed automatically; the DISK LOW pill on the top bar goes up with the room left and the whole warning on hover, written once in those frames; while it stays low a banner posted after it keeps the slot and the pill stays up; when there is room again the pill and its own banner come down, low again is said again, and a banner that has since taken the slot is left up; a drive only one use writes to reads in the singular; a console that has not measured is not taken for a low drive; and every telemetry frame asks, with the pill in the top bar's markup",
  "run_link_control.py":
    "The run and link lifecycle in one arc on one boot: that pause HOLDS a survey rather than losing it (the leg is preserved and resumed, which is the difference between pause and stop), that a transit is arm-gated with its route validated first, that reset is a clean simulator power-cycle and REFUSES on a real link, and that a disconnect leaves no link still taking commands It also holds, in-process, that a telemetry frame read before a command is never applied after it: a Stop, Pause, Start, E-STOP, disarm or Go-To fired between the link's frame and its use reads as that command from the moment it returns - a Stop used to read running and then complete, never stopped. It also holds that a route over the console's waypoint limit is refused whole, in words, and one at the limit passes the route check - the limit the state publishes is the one applied, and it fits the largest plan in the session logs - and that an upload of a saved plan meets the same bound; the console used to keep the first 1000 waypoints and drop the rest. And (review #12) that the telemetry loop survives its own faults: a fault on every tick does not end it, every frame carries loop_fault and the traceback is printed once; telemetry is applied again when it stops and the fault comes down after eight clean ticks, not one; a fault on every other tick stays one fault; a fault in the state snapshot itself publishes nothing and still does not end the loop; a message carrying a value is printed once for its line; a fresh link starts with no fault, with the state saying whether frames are streaming; and a report that cannot be written - the console window gone - neither ends the loop nor brings the fault straight back.",
  "home_spawn.py":
    "That Set-Home lands where it says from EITHER of its two sources: an explicit point is honoured exactly, and with no point given the vessel's own live fix is captured - never a dead link's last telemetry, which once made Home a place the boat no longer was and Return-to-Home a destination taken from a simulation. Also that half a coordinate is refused rather than falling back silently, that a malformed one is a refusal NAMING the rule rather than the generic failure a bare cast would produce, that the console is still answering afterwards at all - a coordinate that reaches the engine does not merely set a bad home, it stops the console - that Return-to-Home closes on the home that was set, and that a spawn is a validated power-cycle at the chosen point",
  "energy_chartinfo.py":
    "The energy override's TWO layers, earned separately because the engine's published gauge masks the simulated tank while the override is on and is the only mechanism when there is no link at all; and that chart info is served from its exact-key cache and answers a malformed area with a usage message rather than a failure",
  "enc_extract.py":
    "The chart-feature extract the whole keep-out model is built from: that a malformed area is refused with usage, that the cache answers without going upstream and is never rewritten by a read, and that the shallow-water retag is per-request with an exclusive boundary. It also guards the query parse and cache key SHARED with the chart-info route, which a mutation must be caught for from both sides",
  "env_water.py":
    "That an environment override REACHES the running vessel rather than only the readout, that disabling it returns the water to calm (every other suite stands on that), and that a non-numeric water-level offset is a stated refusal rather than a dropped connection - that branch runs before the dispatcher's safety net, so its guard has to be local",
  "log_routes.py":
    "That a client event survives a data field whose name collides with the record's own - the log renames it rather than refusing, and the record stays FLAT because playback reads it - that the recording list includes the console's own live session, and that the raw-log route serves nothing but a bare session file from the log directory. The only suite that boots WITH logging on, which is what makes that branch reachable at all",
  "data_routes.py":
    "The remaining data surfaces: that switching vessel is gated to disarmed-and-idle and, when allowed, really takes effect (proved by the energy gauge changing type, which a renamed profile cannot fake) while LEAVING THE BOAT WHERE IT IS - a new hull is not a new location; that the OPERATING PORT is what decides position, that the shipped bases are listed with New Castle NH primary, that switching one moves the boat and adding one is both selected and retained on disk, that a malformed base is a 400 saying what is wrong, and that a suite can never reach the operator's own registry; that the comms password leaks by none of its three paths - never stored, never echoed, never logged - and that the tide and recovery-point routes map their errors rather than failing open",
  "roc_persist.py":
    "That only the most recent few recovery points survive a restart, so the registry cannot grow until a correct Remove button LOOKS broken - the reported fault was a control smothered by 198 stale records, not a control that failed - and that no test suite can write to the operator's own registry",
  "ais_error_frames.py":
    "That an upstream fault SURFACES instead of reading as a quiet sea: the feed reports its errors as ordinary text frames on the data channel, which the ingest path used to discard silently, so an invalid key showed as connected with nothing in sight forever. The error must also survive the periodic quiet-area re-stamp and clear only when real data arrives",
  "ais_sources.py":
    "Many traffic feeds merged into ONE picture: that each vessel carries provenance for every feed that reported it, that a stale polled report cannot walk a live track backwards while its static details still merge, that a feed answering a fault AS DATA on a successful response surfaces as an error, and that a mistyped endpoint is refused loudly rather than binding the wrong thing",
  "station_windows.py":
    "That the tide and weather pages opened alongside the console are for the stations the VESSEL'S OWN FIX selects - no station identifier is hardcoded anywhere, enforced by parsing the source - and that where a reading is blended from several stations the console DISCLOSES the blend rather than reporting it under one station's name",
  "turn_channel.js":
    "That a survey's end-of-line turns may only use navigation-channel water the survey's own coverage lines already occupy. Every layer was individually correct when turns arced into a dredged channel across a charted row of pilings: the channel is only a keep-out when enforced, the line-clipping rule only fires on lines that CROSS it, and the piles are charted far enough apart that a turn could lawfully thread between them",
  "chart_source_card.js":
    "That the chart-source card lists EVERY cell in view, one row each, broadest scale first, with the vessel's own marked - it used to name one and count the rest, hiding the scale and survey age of the sheets a survey was about to work over. Also that a cell arriving as several polygons appears once, and that the vessel's cell is the largest-scale one containing it",
  "ui_tooltips.js":
    "That an informational tip is anchored to its CONTROL rather than to the pointer, flipping above at the bottom of the screen and clamped to the viewport, so the pointer sitting on a control can never sit on the tip explaining it - and that a readout which rewrites its own tip text mid-hover wins over the stashed copy",
  "units_toggle.js":
    "That the distance-unit preference converts the VALUE and not merely the label - a field labeled in nautical miles showing kilometers is wrong by a factor with a plausible number on screen - that it applies only to LONG distances, and that no readout hand-rolls its own kilometer string beside the toggle it would then ignore",
  "measure_tool.js":
    "The chart ruler and the right-click menu that arms it: that the reading flows ALONG the leg and never upside-down, that it goes through the one distance formatter so it follows the display-unit pill, that a bearing is the azimuth from the first click and not its reciprocal, that a pan-drag is never read as a measurement, that each of the menu's vessel commands asks the console's own gate predicate rather than a second copy of the rule, and that Set Home acts at the clicked point AND tests that point against the keep-out model before accepting it quietly - a home the vessel cannot return to is a refusal deferred to mid-mission",
  "chart_ink.js":
    "Reading the CHART ITSELF where the ENC has nothing to say. A finger pier that no vector layer describes is still a pier, and a Go-To drawn through one is a Go-To through a structure; the tiles carry the ink even when the feature service does not. Holds that the image read is bounded to the water the plan actually crosses rather than to the whole view, that what it finds is folded into the SAME keep-out model every behavior already routes against instead of becoming a second parallel obstacle set, and that a read which fails or returns nothing degrades to the vector model with the operator told, never to a silent 'clear'.",
  "gate_endpoint.js":
    "The Rule 9 lane gate's endpoint exemption, and the question it asks. The gate is the law over the lane - no route leaves the pipeline without passing the same legClear the obstacle search obeyed - and it waives one thing: a block within two buffers of the route's own start or goal, because a vessel moored inside the buffer must still be led out and an arrival can end at a dock. The exemption used to test only whether the block was NEAR an endpoint, which says nothing about whether that endpoint is anywhere it should not be; measured over 475 Go-To routes from one spawn, that shipped a route passing 0.47 m from a charted pier under a 3 m buffer, with clear water around both endpoints. It asks whether the ENDPOINT ITSELF is blocked now, and the moored-vessel half is untouched.",
  "line_stats.js":
    "What the Lines card and the run-progress figure are a measure OF. Both quantities reset on the TRANSITION into a running state - but every commanded motion sets that state unconditionally, so a Go-To, a return home or a transit issued while something is already running never leaves it, and nothing resets. The card then indexes per-line timings by POSITION into a line set the operator has since edited, and the only self-heal repaired the array's LENGTH, which is exactly the case striking one line of ten does not produce. Holds that the stats are keyed to the line set itself rather than to its size, and that the run clock and percentage describe the run in front of the operator.",
  "trail_persist.js":
    "That the trail is saved while the boat moves and a long day of it is kept (review #21), driving the page's own trail block, its position append and its flush wiring over a fake clock and a store that can refuse a write: a boat laying a point every 0.75 s is written every TRACK_SAVE_MS with the trail up to each write (a throttle - the old debounce never wrote while points kept coming); a reload mid-run restores every stored point under its boot; a flush writes what is pending at once and nothing when nothing is; a point is laid once the boat is TRACK_STEP_M from the last one and a boat holding station lays none; a run past the cap keeps the newest 80 km; that trail is stored as microdegree deltas at a fraction of the old size, within 0.12 m; a write refused for its size keeps the newest part that fits, and with storage switched off a write is tried once; older stored shapes still load and a damaged one gives back what came before the damage without throwing; a clear cancels the armed write and later points are saved again; the page flushes on pagehide and when hidden, not when shown; and drawing a long trail keeps vertices at least a pixel apart within a pixel and a half of every point, leaves out runs beyond one edge of the view without cutting a corner across it, draws the boat's end, and projects each point once across zooms",
  "spawn_trail.js":
    "The trail belongs to a BOOT, and a respawn ends the boot. The server mints a new boot id on every simulator power-cycle and the browser drops the trail when it changes - but that comparison ran once per page load, so a mid-session respawn never fired it and three call sites were leaning on their own pre-emptive clear, fired BEFORE the command that reboots the simulator. That races the frames it is trying to get ahead of and loses: measured live, the old link's next telemetry frame lands 160 ms after the spawn command, so exactly two points survived and the chart drew a 5 km line between the placement the operator abandoned and the one they chose. Holds that the id is checked on EVERY frame, that a refused spawn costs the operator nothing, and - the acceptance case that pairs with all of it - that within one boot the trail still accumulates.",
  "survey_transit_roles.js":
    "A coverage line is not a transit, and the hop to the next coverage region is not a turn. A survey run is three jobs at three speeds and one classifier decides which is live; this drives the REAL accumulator tick by tick along a two-region plan rather than hand-setting its output, which is the difference that matters - the classifier was never wrong, but the state handed to it could not occur, because an open turn was cleared only on ENTERING a line. Measured live, that flew all 618 m of a region hop at the turn speed, 4.0 kn where the transit speed does it in 84.9 s. The rule is read from the PLAN and not from where the vessel happens to be: a gap between consecutive lines is a reversal within four times the plan's own median end-to-start gap and a transit beyond it, so a gap left by a struck line is still flown as the reversal it is. The committed plan's duration estimate is billed by the same rule, so the estimate and the vessel cannot disagree about what a leg is.",
  "min_depth_floor.js":
    "The operator's Min depth as a ROUTING floor for every behavior, not a survey setting. The floor in force is the DEEPER of it and the hull's own navigability limit, so a value set below what the hull needs can never narrow its clearance - the same rule the keep-clear buffer keeps one control over. The half that must not be 'finished' is the maximum: deep water is not a hazard, the survey Max depth is a COVERAGE window, and enforcing it in the shared model would make a Go-To across a deep channel unroutable. Holds that raising the floor re-classifies charted water that was navigable into keep-outs (measured live: 1148 zones at a 2.3 m hull floor, 1561 at an 8 m operator floor), that a rebuild rather than a re-fetch is enough because each feature carries its own sounding, that the readout prints the EFFECTIVE floor so the zone count beside it can be reconciled, that both controls share one writer, and that the value survives a reload through all four whitelists it has to pass - the client's rebuild and the server's load, save and empty-file default. It also holds that the TIDE is a depth input like the floor: the model records the water level it was built at and is rebuilt once the live level moves TIDE_REBUILD_M from it - measured from the BUILT level, so a station's 5 cm steps add up - through one writer (applyWaterOffset) that runs before the clearance guard reads the model in the same frame. Without it a model built at high water kept a drying flat and a charted rock out of the keep-outs however far the tide fell.",
  "state_dir.py":
    "That no test writes the operator's own files (review #16). A console keeps its plan, comms settings, port registry, ROC registry and session logs beside the program, and seventeen suites started one in the app folder - most of them protecting the plan by reading mission.json when they began and writing it back when they ended, which lost any edit the operator made in their own console meanwhile. Holds that every suite that starts a console passes a state folder of its own (--state-dir, through tests/lib/console_state.py), read out of the source by AST so a suite added later is covered the day it is added; and, on a temp COPY of the program so that even a broken flag cannot reach the operator's files, that a console given the flag reads the plan, comms settings and port registry already in that folder, writes its plan, comms settings, port registry, ROC registry and session log there and nothing beside the program, while the same copy without the flag does write beside itself - so the negative check can see a write there. And (review #27) that every temp folder a suite makes, the suite removes: each mkdtemp is named and the name reaches rmtree or rmSync.",
  "mission_store.py":
    "That the plan file survives concurrency, corruption and speed commands. On Windows a reader holding mission.json open made os.replace fail with WinError 5, a read landing mid-replace was answered with an EMPTY plan, and set_speed saved what it had loaded - so a speed command could lose itself and write an empty plan over a real one. Holds that only a missing file is no plan; a locked file is retried then refused and a corrupt one copied aside and refused; writers and readers together produce no empty plan and no failed save; a replace refused by another process is retried; a speed command writes nothing and a Go-To flies the live speed even with the file gone bad; and each change to the plan geometry keeps the previous plan in .bak1..N, with an empty plan never taking a slot. Runs against a temp path and asserts that nothing it did wrote, replaced or removed the app folder's plan files - recorded as it happens, not a hash compared afterwards, which would fail whenever the operator's own console saved meanwhile. It also holds that a body posted as the plan that is not one is refused in words and writes nothing, while an explicitly empty plan is saved; that every save bumps the plan's revision and an edit made to an older revision is refused naming both, and changes nothing the console runs by - End of Plan stays what the file says - including when the file is locked while its revision is read; that a save carrying no revision is written as before; and, over HTTP with the console's own handler, that every POST route answers - an exception in any of them is a 500 in words, not a dropped connection.",
};

function harnessRows() {
  const dir = path.join(__dirname, "..", "tests");
  return fs.readdirSync(dir).filter(f => /\.(js|py)$/.test(f)).sort().map(f => [
    "`" + (f.endsWith(".js") ? "node" : "python") + " tests/" + f + "`",
    GUARDS[f] || "(undocumented — add an entry to GUARDS in tools/build_tech_manual.js)",
  ]);
}

// ---- content ---------------------------------------------------------------
const c = [];

c.push(TITLE("ASV Simulator"));
c.push(P("Technical Manual — architecture, subsystems, API and extension guide", { size: 24, color: "2e5f8a" }));
c.push(P("A browser-based command console and high-fidelity simulator for autonomous surface vessels. Vendor-neutral by design: the console core models no particular hull, and every vessel-specific parameter lives in a per-vessel configuration file.", { italics: true }));
c.push(SP());

c.push(H1("Contents"));
[
  "1  Introduction and document set",
  "2  System architecture",
  "3  Repository layout",
  "4  Vessel configuration — the single source of truth",
  "5  Server reference (asv_console.py)",
  "6  Client reference (static/asv.html)",
  "7  Behaviors, the nogo model and ENC-aware routing",
  "8  Survey planning and turn geometry",
  "9  Remote Operations Centers and moving HOME",
  "10  HTTP API reference",
  "11  Data formats and persistence",
  "12  Configuration and constants",
  "13  Development practice and verification",
  "14  Known limitations and roadmap",
  "15  Extension guide",
].forEach(t => c.push(B(t)));

// 1 -------------------------------------------------------------------------
c.push(H1("1  Introduction and document set"));
c.push(P("The ASV Simulator is a single-operator command console for an autonomous surface vessel, plus a simulator faithful enough to rehearse a whole mission against real chart data before any hardware is involved. It plans surveys and search patterns on a live nautical chart, routes every leg clear of charted hazards, drives the vessel through an autopilot model that responds to real wind and sea state, and records the session for replay."));
c.push(P("The console is deliberately GENERIC. It models no particular manufacturer's vessel: the onboard controller is referred to throughout as a `VCU` (vessel control unit), and every hull, propulsion, maneuvering, power and planning parameter is read from a vessel configuration file rather than compiled in. Adding a new vessel is a data change, not a code change — see chapter 4."));
c.push(H2("1.1  Document set"));
c.push(P("Four GENERATED documents plus the repository's own notes. The generated set shares one formatting module and is rebuilt with a single command; never hand-edit a generated document."));
c.push(TBL(["Document", "Audience", "Covers"], [
  ["Quick Start (generated)", "First-time users", "Running it, a first commanded behavior, a first survey, in about twenty minutes"],
  ["Operations Manual (generated)", "Operators", "Safety model, display, chart awareness, every behavior, planning depth, contingencies, checklists"],
  ["This manual (generated)", "Engineers, programmers", "Architecture, subsystems, API, formats, constants, extension recipes"],
  ["Development Guide (generated)", "Contributors, maintainers", "How the project is built and verified: testing philosophy, defect shapes, case studies, extension recipes"],
  ["`README.md`", "Operators, new readers", "What it is, running it, and a walkthrough of every feature"],
  ["`README_SIM.md`", "Operators", "The simulation model, command flow, endpoints, mission walkthrough"],
  ["`README_PLAYBACK.md`", "Operators", "Session recording and the read-only playback view"],
  ["`CLAUDE.md`", "Maintainers", "Design decisions, durable gotchas, and the session log from 2026-09-05 on"],
  ["`HANDOFF_ARCHIVE.md`", "Maintainers", "The handoffs and session notes from before 2026-09-05, moved out of CLAUDE.md verbatim"],
], [2400, 2100, 4860]));
c.push(SP());
c.push(P("Rebuild the whole generated set with `cd tools && node build_docs.js`. Each generator is standalone and can be run on its own; the shared formatting lives in `tools/docx_kit.js`."));
c.push(P("Design documents — `PLAN.md`, `ASV_BEHAVIORS_PLAN.md`, `ENC_PUNCHOUT_PLAN.md` — record intent for individual subsystems and are historical rather than normative."));

// 2 -------------------------------------------------------------------------
c.push(H1("2  System architecture"));
c.push(P("Three processes, one browser, and no framework. The console is stdlib-only Python serving a single self-contained HTML page; the only child processes are optional services."));
c.push(CODE([
  "  browser (static/asv.html)          the whole UI: chart, planning, command bar",
  "     |  HTTP + SSE (/api/state)",
  "  asv_console.py                     server: engine, mission store, ENC, water, env",
  "     |  VcuLink (SimVcu | RealVcu)",
  "  the vessel                         simulated, or a real VCU over TCP/serial",
  "",
  "  ais_service.py    (child)          AIS traffic provider, proxied at /api/ais",
  "  gps_sim.py        (child)          NMEA-0183 GPS stream for a ROC position feed",
]));
c.push(H2("2.1  The honest seam"));
c.push(P("`VcuLink` is the interface between the console and the vessel. `SimVcu` implements it fully — hull dynamics, autopilot, power model, waypoint following. `RealVcu` implements the same interface and REFUSES every command with a clear error, because the wire protocol for a real controller is not implemented. This is deliberate: an unimplemented real link that silently accepted commands would be far more dangerous than one that says so."));
c.push(H2("2.2  Where logic lives"));
c.push(P("Route planning, chart clipping and survey geometry run in the BROWSER, not the server. The client owns the ENC feature set and computes every route against it, then uploads the finished waypoint list. One consequence matters and is easy to trip over: a raw `POST /api/cmd/upload` with no `route` bypasses ENC routing entirely, because the routing code is client-side JavaScript. Scripted headless missions must therefore either supply a pre-routed path or stay in open water."));
c.push(H2("2.3  Single-file by decision"));
c.push(P("The server is one Python file and the client is one HTML file. This is a settled decision, not an accident: it keeps deployment to a copy, keeps the whole system greppable, and removes the build step. The sibling console split its routing into a separate `routing.js`; this one deliberately did not, and the regression harnesses extract functions directly out of `static/asv.html` on that assumption."));

// 3 -------------------------------------------------------------------------
c.push(H1("3  Repository layout"));
c.push(TBL(["Path", "Purpose"], [
  ["`asv_console.py`", "The server: HTTP + SSE, Engine, VcuLink/SimVcu/RealVcu, mission store, ENC fetch, water level, environment, vessel configuration, session recorder"],
  ["`static/asv.html`", "The entire client: chart, planning, routing, behaviors, all cards"],
  ["`static/playback.html`", "Read-only session playback view"],
  ["`roc_tracks.py`", "Remote Operations Center tracking, GPS ingest and moving HOME"],
  ["`gps_sim.py`", "NMEA-0183 GPS data-stream simulator (TCP server or UDP)"],
  ["`ais_service.py`", "Standalone AIS traffic provider, started as a child process - and started again by a watchdog if it exits, after a wait that doubles on each failure (review #27)"],
  ["`vessels/*.json`", "One self-contained configuration per modeled vessel"],
  ["`tests/`", "Headless regression harnesses (stdlib Node / stdlib Python)"],
  ["`tools/`", "Documentation generators (this manual)"],
  ["`charts/`", "Chart tile and ENC caches (generated at runtime, not versioned)"],
  ["`logs/`", "Session recordings (JSONL) and child-process logs"],
], [2600, 6760]));

// 4 -------------------------------------------------------------------------
c.push(H1("4  Vessel configuration — the single source of truth"));
c.push(P("This chapter describes the architectural feature that most distinguishes this console: it studies ASV behavior across vessel TYPES, so no vessel parameter may be hardcoded anywhere."));
c.push(P("Every hull, windage, propulsion, maneuvering, autopilot, power, planning and spawn value for a modeled vessel lives in exactly one place — `vessels/<id>.json`. The server validates it at load, publishes it to module globals, and serves it to the client at `GET /api/vessel`. Server and client therefore agree by construction."));
c.push(H2("4.1  Lifecycle"));
c.push(B("`load_vessel(id)` reads `vessels/<id>.json`."));
c.push(B("`validate_vessel(v)` checks it against a field schema. A missing or mistyped field fails the load with a clear, path-pointed error — a bad file never runs with placeholder physics."));
c.push(B("`apply_vessel(v)` publishes the values to the module globals that `SimVcu`, the mission store and the planners read."));
c.push(B("The client's `loadVessel()` fetches `/api/vessel` and mirrors the same values into its own constants (speeds, turn rate, nogo buffer, minimum navigable depth, search-pattern sizes) and the energy gauge."));
c.push(P("`--vessel <id>` selects the vessel at start. `POST /api/vessel {id}` switches it live; the switch is gated on disarmed and idle, returns 409 otherwise, and respawns the simulated vessel at the new vessel's spawn point."));
c.push(H2("4.2  The staleness rule"));
c.push(P("EVERY value derived from the vessel must be re-derived inside `apply_vessel()`. A constant computed once at import goes stale the moment the operator switches vessel in the top-bar picker, and the resulting bug is subtle — the console keeps running, with one subsystem quietly modelling the previous hull. Historic instances: the underwater lateral area used for leeway drag, and the ROC recovery standoff."));
c.push(H2("4.3  Schema"));
c.push(TBL(["Block", "Fields", "Drives"], [
  ["`hull`", "`loa_m`, `beam_m`, `above_water_h_m`, `draft_m`, `wind_cd`, `hull_cd`", "Windage silhouettes, leeway drag, minimum navigable depth, ROC recovery standoff"],
  ["`propulsion`", "`speeds_kn.{low,survey,high}`", "Plan speeds, ETA estimates, turn radius, moving-HOME closing check"],
  ["`maneuvering`", "`max_turn_rate_deg_s`, `approach_m`, `lookahead_m`, `arrival_radius_m`", "Minimum turn radius, survey turn geometry, waypoint following"],
  ["`autopilot`", "`xte_ki_deg`, `xte_i_max_deg`", "Cross-track integral term under a steady sideways push"],
  ["`power`", "`type` = `battery` (voltage sag) or `fuel` (litres, burn curve)", "Endurance, range, the console's energy gauge"],
  ["`planning`", "`nogo_buffer_m`, `under_keel_clearance_m`, optional `channel_reach_m`, optional `min_survey_line_m`, `roc`, `search`", "Keep-out buffer, depth floor, channel lane reach, shortest survey line worth running for this hull, ROC defaults, search sizes"],
  ["`display`", "optional `hull_color`, `hull_color2`", "The vessel marker's color on the chart - the hull's own livery. Absent falls back to the chart default, so an older profile is unchanged"],
  ["`spawn`", "`lat`, `lon`", "Where the simulated vessel comes up WHEN NO OPERATING PORT IS SELECTED - a port, if there is one, decides position (see 4.5)"],
], [1500, 3700, 4160]));
c.push(SP());
c.push(P("The minimum navigable depth is DERIVED, not declared: `draft_m + under_keel_clearance_m`. A deep-draft vessel therefore treats more water as nogo automatically, and the client re-extracts the chart on a vessel switch so the keep-out model follows."));
c.push(H2("4.5  Operating ports (ports.json)"));
c.push(P("A PORT IS WHERE YOU ARE; THE VESSEL IS WHAT YOU ARE DRIVING. They were one thing until the port registry existed: every vessel file carried its own spawn, so choosing a hull chose a location, and the console could not be pointed at another base without editing a vessel's configuration. Ports are now their own registry, in the same shape as the vessel picker - a list, an active id, and a switch permitted only while disarmed and idle, because moving the base under a running boat is as incoherent as swapping its physics."));
c.push(TBL(["Field", "Value", "Drives"], [
  ["`id`", "stable key, derived from the name when absent", "What `--base` and the picker refer to"],
  ["`name`", "display name", "What the operator reads in the dropdown"],
  ["`lat` / `lon`", "decimal degrees", "The chart's opening view, and where the simulated vessel spawns"],
  ["`ofs`", "optional NOAA Operational Forecast System id", "Which regional current model covers this base. A model is REGIONAL: the wrong one answers with an error rather than a current"],
  ["`note`", "free text", "Why this position and not another - siting, charted depth, known limits"],
], [1500, 3700, 4160]));
c.push(SP());
c.push(P("The active port has the last word on the spawn: apply_port() runs at the end of apply_vessel(), so a hull's own spawn is only the fallback for a console with no port selected. A vessel switch therefore does NOT move the boat, and a port switch does."));
c.push(P("A port can be created FROM A NAME. Posting a name with no position geocodes it, and then - the half that matters - snaps the result to charted water deep enough for the selected hull. A geocoder returns the center of a TOWN, and a town center is on land: a well known Alaskan port resolves to a street corner, and the console walks outward through the chart's own depth areas until it finds a berth the vessel can float in. Where no navigable water is charted nearby the port is still created at the place center, but flagged unverified and carrying the reason - never presented as a berth. A name matching nothing is refused rather than becoming a port at sea."));
c.push(P("Ports the operator adds are RETAINED. The picker's add entry saves the chart's current center under a typed name and writes it back to the registry, so a base entered once is in the list from then on - the gesture carries the point, exactly as the chart menu's Go-To, Set Home and Spawn do, rather than asking anyone to read a latitude off the screen and type it back in."));
c.push(P("A suite that touches ports MUST keep the registry out of the application directory - every suite that starts a console now gives it a state folder of its own with --state-dir, which moves ports.json with the rest of its state; --ports-config still names a single file. Switching or adding SAVES, so a test run against the application directory would rewrite the operator's own bases - the same accumulation that once put 198 stale records into the recovery-point registry."));
c.push(SP());
c.push(H2("4.4  Adding a vessel"));
c.push(P("Write `vessels/<id>.json` with every required field, start with `--vessel <id>`, and confirm the load message. There is no code to change. If a new subsystem needs a vessel-dependent value, add the field to the schema with a SENSIBLE DERIVED FALLBACK so that vessel files written before the field existed continue to load — see the ROC recovery standoff in chapter 9 for the pattern."));

// 5 -------------------------------------------------------------------------
c.push(H1("5  Server reference (asv_console.py)"));
c.push(H2("5.1  Engine"));
c.push(P("`Engine` owns the link, the armed/e-stop state, the run state machine and the telemetry loop. It ticks the link, absorbs telemetry into a state snapshot, and pushes that snapshot to every SSE subscriber. It also resolves HOME on every tick (chapter 9) and drives the moving-HOME chase. It asks the link for each frame outside its lock, so a real link's read never delays a command - which means a command can land between a frame's read and its use. Every command that changes the run bumps a generation number, and a frame read before one is dropped rather than applied: applied, it put the run back the way it was, so a Stop read running and then complete, never stopped."));
c.push(P("THE TELEMETRY LOOP SURVIVES ITS OWN FAULTS, AND SAYS SO. An exception anywhere in a tick - a monitor fed a position, the state snapshot, the moving-HOME chase - used to end the loop's thread: telemetry stopped, and because the event stream keeps its connection alive with comments, the page's link dot stayed green over readouts that no longer moved. `_run` now restarts the loop on the next tick and records the fault on the state as `loop_fault` - the latest error, a count, and when the episode began. Each distinct raising line is printed with its traceback and logged once per episode - keyed on the line, not the message, because a message carrying a value differs on every tick - and the fault is taken down only after `LOOP_FAULT_CLEAR_TICKS` (8, two seconds) clean ticks in a row, so a fault that fires on every other tick stays one fault. A fault in the state snapshot itself cannot be published at all; the loop still survives it, and the page's stale check covers the silence. The handler's own reports are guarded as well: a write to a console window that has gone raises, and raised inside the handler it would end the loop after all. The state also carries `streaming`, true while a link exists - the loop publishes a frame on every tick then, healthy or lost - and that is what the page keys TELEMETRY STALE to. A fresh link starts with no fault."));
c.push(P("Run states are `idle`, `running`, `paused`, `stopped` and `complete`. Behaviors are `survey`, `goto`, `rth`, `transit`, `hold` and `escape`. Completion modes are `rth`, `complete`, `loiter` and `repeat`."));
c.push(H3("5.1.1  End-of-plan setting versus run completion"));
c.push(P("Two different things, deliberately kept in two different places. The END-OF-PLAN SETTING is the operator's persistent choice of what happens when a survey or search PLAN finishes; it is owned by the mission store and read through a cached accessor that every mission read and write refreshes. NO ENGINE METHOD WRITES IT. The RUN COMPLETION is what the run currently in progress does at its own end — a Go-To, return-to-home, hold or transit all station-keep at their endpoint, so a Go-To legitimately runs as “loiter”."));
c.push(P("They were one field once. Because a Go-To correctly set that field to loiter, commanding one silently overwrote the operator's end-of-plan selection: the command bar still showed the selection while the console acted on loiter, and the end-of-plan return-to-home chain, which gates on that value, was disarmed until the next plan start re-read the mission. Both values are now published on the state, the client reads the setting for the selector and the chain and the run value for the mode readout, and the selector mirrors the server's setting so the two cannot drift apart unnoticed."));
c.push(H3("5.1.2  ... and the END ACTION, which is what the operator asked"));
c.push(P("Neither field answers the question the cards are actually being read for: WHERE DOES THIS RUN LEAVE THE BOAT? The link holds at the last waypoint for both “loiter” and “rth”, and “rth” only becomes a return home because the CLIENT chains one on seeing that hold. So a Go-To or transit under End of Plan = RTH reported run completion “loiter” and every readout said the boat would stay at its endpoint — right up to the moment it turned for home. Each field was accurate about itself and wrong about the boat."));
c.push(P("`endAction()` in the client is the third value, and the only one the cards show. It is the run's own completion, upgraded to “rth” when the end-of-plan chain will fire, and downgraded from “rth” to “loiter” when it cannot: the chain needs a home, arming, no E-STOP, a run that actually ends (a repeat run laps forever and a complete run stops rather than holding), the LIVE setting rather than the frozen uploaded one, and a route home that was not refused. A card that promises a return the boat is not going to make is worse than one that never mentioned it."));
c.push(P("The chain is a one-shot per commanded run, and what re-arms it is the boat being UNDER WAY — not a run starting. The console lets a second Go-To be commanded while the first is still running, so the run state never leaves “running” and an idle-to-running edge never fires; that second run inherited a spent chain and held at its endpoint indefinitely. Invisible until the cards began naming the end state, at which point one sat there advertising a return home for five minutes."));
c.push(H2("5.2  SimVcu"));
c.push(P("The simulator: waypoint following by look-ahead line-of-sight guidance, a yaw-rate-capped heading response, a speed model, and a power model that is either battery voltage sag or diesel fuel burn depending on the vessel file. Wind and sea state from the environment monitor push it off track, and the guidance answers with a crab feedforward plus a cross-track integral term, the way a real autopilot does."));
c.push(H2("5.3  Supporting services"));
c.push(B("`CommsMonitor` — the radio link status readout."));
c.push(B("`WaterLevel` — real-time water level from public tide stations, ADDED to charted depths so the nogo model reflects the tide now, not the chart datum."));
c.push(P("THE MONITORS DO NOT FREEZE, AND THEIR READINGS CARRY AN AGE. `WaterLevel`, `EnvMonitor` and `CurrentsMonitor` each run their loop body through `_monitor_pass`. None of the three loops had a handler: one exception ended the thread and the reading froze, still marked ok and carrying no time - reproduced with an `http.client.IncompleteRead`, a connection cut mid-body, which `_coops_get` did not catch (it does now, and a station cache that is not JSON is fetched again). A pass that raises is recorded as `_error`, published as `monitor_error`, and printed when its text changes; the last good reading is kept and the next completed pass clears the error. Each snapshot carries `age_s`: the water level from the station's own observation time `t`, the weather from the oldest buoy observation in the blend (`obs_t`, read off the NDBC row), and the current from when it was computed. The current is a forecast for now, so it is recomputed from the cached cycle every `SAMPLE_S` (60 s) while the cycle itself is looked for every `POLL_S`, or at once on a move, a refresh or a model switch - and when no cycle can be had, the reading carries `_ensure_cycle`'s reason instead of \u201cno cycle cached yet\u201d, which used to overwrite it on the same pass. Each loop clears its wake-up flag only when a wait was actually woken by it: clearing after a wait that timed out swallowed a refresh, or a move, that landed between the two calls, until the next poll. The reason is kept from the last look through every sample until the next one; kept only on the pass that looked, the live card went back to \u201cno cycle cached yet\u201d a minute later."));
c.push(B("`EnvMonitor` — real wind and wave observations near the vessel, feeding the simulator's environmental forcing. Simulation only."));
c.push(B("`RocTracker` — Remote Operations Centers and HOME selection (chapter 9)."));
c.push(B("`SessionLogger` — append-only JSONL recording of every command, setting and telemetry sample."));

// 6 -------------------------------------------------------------------------
c.push(H1("6  Client reference (static/asv.html)"));
c.push(P("One page, one module script scope - its lowest layer imported from `static/js/` - and no build step. It owns the slippy chart, the ENC feature set, all planning geometry, all route search, and every card."));
c.push(H2("6.1  Cards"));
c.push(TBL(["Card", "Purpose"], [
  ["Survey (`SURV`)", "Three-click survey pattern, spacing/direction, Punch Out, Add to plan"],
  ["Survey area (`BND`)", "Arbitrary boundary polygon that clips the pattern"],
  ["Search (`SRCH`)", "Canned search patterns: expanding box, sector, parallel track"],
  ["Transit (`TRAN`)", "Draw and follow a route with no survey plan"],
  ["Mission status", "Live telemetry, water level, nogo, comms — and the RUN block: one readout for every commanded run (type, waypoint progress, routed length, time to end, end mode, run time, status), shown only while there is a run to describe"],
  ["ROC · HOME", "Remote Operations Centers, recovery offsets, and HOME selection"],
  ["Chart source (`SRC`)", "Every ENC cell in view + the zone-of-confidence under the vessel"],
  ["Survey lines (`LINES`)", "Per-line planned versus actual run times - one row per DRAWN line, a line a keep-out cut read as its parts (`drawnLines` / `lineNo`)"],
  ["AIS", "Nearby traffic from the AIS service"],
], [2200, 7160]));
c.push(H2("6.2  Multi-monitor split"));
c.push(P("The console can run as two windows: a chart window and a controls window (`?panel=controls`), talking over a same-origin BroadcastChannel. The chart window is the single source of truth and owns all logic; the controls window renders no application logic, mirrors the control DOM, and forwards gestures back so every existing handler runs exactly once."));
c.push(P("A THIRD window is opened on the NOAA CO-OPS water-levels page for the station driving the depth correction. The station id is DERIVED from the vessel fix, never configured — `tide_station_url()` is the single place the URL is built, and no station id appears as a literal anywhere in the console. It is opened on its own daemon thread rather than beside the other two, because at process start there is no GPS fix and therefore no station; the thread waits for the water monitor to resolve one and gives up after TIDE_WINDOW_WAIT_S rather than living forever. `--no-tide-window` suppresses it, and `--browser none` opens nothing at all, which is why the test harnesses are unaffected by it."));
c.push(P("The page takes ONE station id, so when the correction is an inverse-distance blend of up to WATER_K stations it shows the PRIMARY — the nearest one actually returning data, the same station the chart-source card attributes the correction to, both reading `water.station` so the two cannot disagree. Because a single station named against a blended number would misrepresent it, the console prints every contributor with its percentage share when the window opens, and the card lists them in its Correction tooltip. Measured at the DriX spawn: Lewes 3.7 km at 95.5%, Brandywine Shoal Light 22.3 km at 2.6%, Cape May 26.4 km at 1.9% — inverse-SQUARE weighting means a near station dominates a far one heavily."));
c.push(P("A FOURTH window is the same mechanism pointed at the other network: the NDBC page for the weather buoy nearest the vessel. Both are entries in one STATION_WINDOWS registry — each naming its snapshot source, its URL builder, its IDW power and its reach — driven by a single open_station_window() that waits, times out and never raises. The hard part is identical for both (there is no station until the vessel has a fix), so a second copy of that loop would only drift. Measured at the DriX spawn, the two networks resolve to the same three sites: LWSD1 3.7 km, BRND1 22.3 km, CMAN4 26.4 km, matching the CO-OPS trio because NDBC carries the co-located gauges."));
c.push(P("CONSEQUENCE FOR NEW UI: every interactive element needs a stable, unique `id`. The bridge forwards controls by id, so an id-less control is inert in the controls window. Handlers should key off class or `data-*` attributes so they stay id-agnostic."));
c.push(H2("6.3  Telemetry health"));
c.push(P("Every telemetry frame runs the clearance guard, the speed governor, the re-approach and the end-of-plan chain inside `onState`, and the page used to catch and drop every error in it - a guard that had stopped running looked exactly like one with nothing to do. Frames now go through `onFrame`, and `consoleHealth` runs on every frame and once a second, so it follows each condition both ways. It says three things on the shared banner, and takes down only a banner of its own:"));
c.push(TBL(["Banner", "When", "Clears"], [
  ["TELEMETRY STALE", "No frame for `STALE_AFTER_MS` (2 s) while the last state said `streaming`. The link dot turns red and the pill beside it reads `stale`. Nothing about a fault is said then, because the page holds only the last frame.", "The next frame"],
  ["CONSOLE FAULT (this page)", "`FRAME_FAULT_AFTER` (3) frames failed in `onState`; the banner names the latest error, the first failure goes to the browser console, and the streak is logged once to the session log as `client:page_frame_fault`.", "`FRAME_CLEAR_AFTER` (8) clean frames in a row"],
  ["CONSOLE FAULT (the console)", "The state carries `loop_fault`: the count, the time it began and the latest error. Said after the page's own fault when both hold.", "When the console clears it"],
], [2200, 5160, 2000]));

c.push(H2("6.4  Page and program versions"));
c.push(P("The page and its modules are read from disk on every request, but the Python only when the console starts - so after a commit or an edit while a console ran, a refresh paired a new page with the old program: fields the page sent were dropped, routes it called answered 404, and nothing said why. `BuildWatch` fingerprints the console's own files - `BUILD_PY` (the console, `currents.py`, `roc_tracks.py`, `ais_service.py`, `gps_sim.py`), `static/*.html` and `static/js/*.js` - by content, with line endings normalized and names taken relative to the program's folder, so a copy elsewhere or a checkout that only rewrites line endings is the same version. It re-reads the files only when a size or modification time has changed, and looks no more often than every `BUILD_RECHECK_S` (5 s)."));
c.push(P("The state carries `build` (the fingerprint when the process started) and `build_on_disk` (now). The page is served with the fingerprint of the files as it was read, filled into `PAGE_BUILD`. `buildCheck`, on every state frame, shows the top-bar pill: RESTART CONSOLE when `build` differs from `build_on_disk`, or when the state has no `build` at all - a console from before this, which a page that knows to ask is newer than; RELOAD PAGE when a filled-in `PAGE_BUILD` differs from `build`; and nothing otherwise, including for a page that was not served by a console, whose `PAGE_BUILD` still holds the token."));

// 7 -------------------------------------------------------------------------
c.push(H1("7  Behaviors, the nogo model and ENC-aware routing"));
c.push(P("Every behavior is chart-aware and arm-gated. Nothing moves the vessel until the operator arms."));
c.push(H2("7.1  The nogo model"));
c.push(P("Chart features are fetched from public electronic navigational chart services and reduced to keep-outs: shoreline, docks and piers, charted hazards, obstructions, and any water shallower than the vessel's derived minimum navigable depth. Charted depths are corrected by the live water level first. The result is a shared model that every behavior plans against, dilated by the vessel's nogo buffer."));
c.push(H3("7.1.1  Reporting the model's state"));
c.push(P("The vessel-status readout NAMES THE STATE rather than printing a count: reading the chart (with the elapsed seconds, so a chart service that has stopped answering does not look like a slow first fetch), a zone count with the vessel's own depth floor and a breakdown by keep-out kind, clear water, or the reason there is no model. The last two are the pair that matters. “The chart was read and there is nothing to avoid here” and “nothing has been checked, so every route is direct and unverified” both present as zero keep-outs and mean opposite things; only the second is a warning, and the readout must not let them look alike."));
c.push(P("Every state is derived from values the model already holds — whether an extract is in flight, whether a successful extract has ever landed, the reason recorded when one did not, and the keep-out counts — so there is no separate display state to drift out of step with what the behaviors route against."));
c.push(H2("7.2  Trusting the water level"));
c.push(P("Charted depths are corrected by the live water level before anything is classified as too shallow, so that correction is part of the safety model rather than a readout. A tide gauge reports the water level near ITS OWN station, and the console interpolates the nearest few \u2014 but if the nearest is hundreds of kilometers away, the number is another coast's tide."));
c.push(P("The reading is therefore banded by the distance to the nearest contributing station. Within a few tens of kilometers it is treated as local and applied normally. Further out it is applied but shown GHOSTED, because tidal range and phase drift materially over that distance. Beyond the remote threshold it is shown ghosted with an explicit warning and NOT APPLIED AT ALL: routing falls back to chart datum, exactly as it does when there is no data. Crediting the vessel with depth nobody has measured in that area is the same failure mode as trusting a charted symbol without its extent."));
c.push(P("The reading is also judged by its AGE. The monitor that keeps it could die and leave its last reading standing, still added to every charted depth, so the reading carries `age_s` from the station's own observation time. Measured across forty sessions (5,693 readings) a live level is 4.6 to 17.1 minutes old; past `WATER_STALE_S` (25 minutes) `waterTrust` marks it stale and `effectiveWaterOffset` does not apply it, whatever its distance band. A manual override is never stale, and a reading with no age at all - an older console - is not called stale: unknown is not old."));
c.push(P("The nearest station decides the banding, not the average of the contributors: an interpolation dominated by a close station is still local, and averaging would both discredit good readings and let two distant stations either side average into a falsely local one. An operator's manual override is always applied and never ghosted \u2014 it is their own number."));
c.push(H2("7.3  Charted hazards have an extent"));
c.push(P("A wreck symbol on a chart is a POSITION, not a size. The casualty beneath it can be a hundred meters long, and the electronic chart says nothing about its extent or which way it lies. Treating such a feature as a bare point and relying on the nogo buffer for clearance models a wrecked ship as a buffer-wide dot — a route then only has to miss the charted position by the buffer to validate clear."));
c.push(P("So hazards whose extent the chart does not give — wrecks, hulks, obstructions and awash rocks — carry an intrinsic radius, defaulting to 50 m and configurable per vessel, and the buffer is added ON TOP of it as the margin it was always meant to be. Objects that genuinely are point-sized, such as piles, buoys and beacons, are unaffected."));
c.push(P("Where the chart gives a sounding OVER the hazard, and that sounding — corrected to the live water level, exactly as depth areas are — clears the vessel's own navigability floor with margin, the vessel can pass over it and the hazard collapses back to a point. THE ABSENCE OF A SOUNDING MEANS UNKNOWN, and unknown takes the full berth rather than the benefit of the doubt."));
c.push(P("Both clearance paths honour this, and both must: the exact leg check that validates a planned path, AND the occupancy raster the route search runs over. If only the exact check knew about the extent, the search would plan straight through the hazard and the leg would simply fail — the vessel would get a refusal instead of a detour. A sized hazard also draws the circle the router keeps out of, so the operator can see why a route swings wide instead of reading a bare symbol as the whole danger."));
c.push(H2("7.4  Route search"));
c.push(B("Direct: an exact clearance check on the straight leg. If clear, done."));
c.push(B("`routeAroundSeg` — a fine sectioned search over a rasterized occupancy grid."));
c.push(B("Escalating swing regions when the detour leaves the search window — the grid coarsens, but the raster OVER-approximates keep-outs, so any coarse solution is genuinely clear and is then refined leg by leg."));
c.push(B("Fine-escape composition when a tight basin closes over the start or the goal: generate open-water escape candidates around the pinched end, route to one, then escalate from open water."));
c.push(P("The whole ladder runs under a time budget. An exhausted budget or a genuine absence of any clear path is reported as unroutable and refused, with the obstruction named. The console never plans a leg it has not verified."));
c.push(H2("7.5  The channel lane (COLREGS Rule 9)"));
c.push(P("In a narrow channel or fairway the vessel keeps to the starboard side. The lane is a purely geometric rule: offset to starboard of the channel centerline, half way out to the edge on that side — a quarter of the full width in from the edge. Buoy COLOR is never an input; it falls out, because lateral marks sit on fixed sides. Opposing traffic therefore passes port to port."));
c.push(P("This applies to every mode — go-to, return-to-home, transit, the survey approach leg, inter-line transits, search transits and any routed detour. Survey coverage lines and generated survey turns are NEVER offset: they are planned geometry and must be run as planned."));
c.push(P("Where the lane lets go. A channel does not end at its last buoy, and neither may the lane: a vessel that releases at the final mark cuts back across the fairway exactly where converging traffic expects it to stand on. The channel centerline is therefore EXTENDED, straight along its own terminal axis, before the lane is built on it — as far as the charted channel polygon continues, or one full channel width past the final pair, whichever reaches further, and capped so a dredged area running far beyond the buoyage cannot drag the lane along water the marks never claimed. The offset is held at full value through that extension and released only after it, entering and leaving alike."));
c.push(P("What the console claims about it. Only one buoy system is laned per leg, so a route running down two successive channels rides the second on its centerline rather than to starboard of it. A plan therefore reports not just whether a lane was ridden but whether it was ridden over ALL of the route: a lane interrupted by a rescued or re-routed stretch, or a second channel left un-laned, is reported as PARTIAL. A Rule 9 banner over a route that is not keeping right is worse than no banner, because it is a claim the operator would otherwise have verified."));
c.push(P("Every route is flyable as well as lawful. Where the clearance gate splices a re-routed patch into the lane, the seam can fold a reversal only a few meters long — a waypoint demanding a near-180-degree turn in less water than any hull can turn in, which a vessel under way answers by orbiting the waypoint until it happens to capture it. The lane producer therefore prunes its final output: any waypoint whose corner exceeds what a transit needs AND whose neighbors connect clear of every keep-out is dropped. The clearance condition means the prune can never straighten a corner that exists to avoid an obstacle — it removes only the folds that no vessel could follow anyway."));

// 8 -------------------------------------------------------------------------
c.push(H1("8  Survey planning and turn geometry"));
c.push(H2("8.1  Pipeline"));
c.push(P("A three-click pattern (start corner, opposite corner, then a spacing and direction point) generates parallel lines filling the box. A center MOVE GRIP translates every anchor by one delta, so the whole pattern moves with shape, spacing and direction preserved exactly. An optional boundary polygon clips the lines. Punch Out then trims everything to chart-clear water:"));
c.push(B("Clip every line to nogo-clear water, sampling at buffer resolution."));
c.push(B("Re-order the result so each obstacle-free region is run as its own serpentine and no leg is numbered through a keep-out."));
c.push(B("Shorten each segment at both ends to settle on-line and leave turning room."));
c.push(B("Drop any segment shorter than this hull's minimum survey line and bridge straight to the next one. A line costs two turns whatever its length, so below some length the vessel spends longer manoeuvring onto it than surveying it — and that length is a property of the hull, so it is vessel configuration (planning.min_survey_line_m), not a constant. The length judged is the segment AS RUN, after the turn margin has been taken off both ends, because that is the water actually surveyed. Zero, or an absent setting, keeps every line: a small survey launch is built for exactly the short lines a large vessel should skip. Whatever is dropped is reported in the punch-out readout with the count and the threshold — coverage is never removed silently. Surveys only; transits, search patterns and hand-drawn lines are never filtered."));
c.push(B("Generate a turn at every line-to-line reversal (below)."));
c.push(B("Route the remaining transits: straight if clear, else around the obstacle with the channel lane applied, else flag them."));
c.push(H2("8.2  Turn geometry"));
c.push(P("Every generated turn is built at a radius the vessel can actually HOLD at the plan speed — derived from the vessel file's maximum turn rate, with a margin for line-following overshoot. There are two shapes, and both are validated against the nogo model before use."));
c.push(TBL(["Shape", "When", "Geometry"], [
  ["Semicircle", "Line offset ≥ 2 × minimum radius", "One 180° arc of radius half the offset, bulging outboard; reaches exactly that radius past the line ends"],
  ["Teardrop", "Line offset < 2 × minimum radius", "Three tangent circles AT the minimum radius: a short arc away from the next line, a >180° loop over the top, a short arc onto the line"],
], [1500, 2800, 5060]));
c.push(SP());
c.push(P("DECOUPLING THE TURN RADIUS FROM THE LINE SPACING is the point of the second shape. A large, slow-turning vessel may need more radius than half the line spacing can ever supply; before the teardrop existed, such a vessel got no turn at all and the plan fell back to a straight hop between anti-parallel line ends — which is a 180° reversal at half the spacing, precisely the radius that had just been rejected as unflyable, only now unmodeled."));
c.push(P("The teardrop costs outboard water: up to roughly 2.75 times the minimum radius past the line ends, against one radius for the semicircle. Punch Out therefore reports the actual excursion, the spacing a plain semicircle would need at the plan speed, and the spacing needed at low speed, so widening the lines or slowing down stays the operator's decision. Where even the teardrop cannot fit clear of the keep-outs, the reversal falls back to a straight hop and the banner marks it UNTRACKABLE rather than clear."));
c.push(P("At exactly twice the minimum radius the teardrop's middle circle degenerates and the two shapes are the same semicircle, so the families agree on their shared boundary."));
c.push(P("TURN WATER. The keep-out set the turns are validated against is STRICTER than the one the transits use: charted channel polygons (ENC dredged areas, plus buoy-gate fairway corridors) that contain no sample of any clipped coverage line are added as keep-outs for the reversal turns, the reversal straight-hop check and the serpentine ordering. The lines' own water is the plan's declared water; a loop may not enter a navigation channel the survey does not occupy, even though a dredged area is deep, is not an enforced keep-out, and a charted pile row (individual point features) cannot block a loop that passes between the points. Routed region-hop transits keep the ordinary keep-out set — crossing a channel to reach the other side is lawful navigation and rides the channel-lane machinery. The grant is per polygon: a survey inside a channel keeps each fragment its lines actually reach. A refusal names the channel in the banner, outlines it as a violation, and quotes the standoff the turns need."));

// 9 -------------------------------------------------------------------------
c.push(H1("9  Remote Operations Centers and moving HOME"));
c.push(P("The operational paradigm: humans command the vessel from one or more Remote Operations Centers. A ROC has a position and a telemetry link. Two kinds exist and a mission may use both."));
c.push(B("SHORE ROC — near the launch harbor, or anywhere. A usually fixed point. The real launch and recovery point is OFFSET from the antenna by an operator-entered range and bearing."));
c.push(B("SHIP ROC (mothership) — aboard a vessel near the mission area. A MOVING point. Recovery happens at a range and bearing FROM the ship, so the offset bearing is normally taken RELATIVE to the ship's course."));
c.push(H2("9.1  Arrival point"));
c.push(P("Each ROC's arrival point is its position walked out along its offset. A `true` offset is a compass bearing; a `relative` offset is measured from the ship's course, so “50 m astern” stays astern as the ship turns. The operator selects one ROC as HOME; the Engine resolves it every telemetry tick, so when HOME is a ship it MOVES and return-to-home chases it."));
c.push(H2("9.2  Lifecycle"));
c.push(P("PLACE → EDIT → CONFIRM. A ROC is created by clicking the chart and comes up STAGED: its position, and for a ship its heading and speed, are edited while it sits still. CONFIRM makes it ACTIVE — a shore ROC becomes a usable HOME anchor, a ship starts steaming. HOLD stops a ship and returns it to STAGED. Only an ACTIVE ROC may be selected as HOME; the gate is a safety property, not decoration."));
c.push(H2("9.3  The chase"));
c.push(P("While a return-to-home follows a ROC, the run loop re-aims the vessel at the ROC's current arrival point, re-issuing a fresh single-waypoint plan only once the point has drifted past about half the arrival radius. This reuses the link's own waypoint following rather than introducing a separate pursuit controller. Any command that leaves the return-to-home run clears the chase."));
c.push(P("A ROC home is driven direct rather than by a chart-routed detour, because a detour computed to a moving recovery point would be stale by the time the vessel arrived."));
c.push(H2("9.4  Vessel-dependent behavior"));
c.push(P("Two ROC values are derived from the active vessel and re-derived on a live vessel switch:"));
c.push(B("RECOVERY STANDOFF — the default astern offset for a new ship ROC scales with the hull (six lengths, with a small-boat floor) and may be overridden outright by `planning.roc.ship_recovery_m`. A fixed distance cannot serve both a two-meter vessel and a twenty-meter one."));
c.push(B("CLOSING CHECK — a return-to-home against a moving mothership only converges if the vessel can overhaul it. The console subtracts the ship's speed from the vessel's best speed and reports the closing rate; below a small margin the recovery point is marked unreachable and the run note says so. The same mothership speed is routine for a fast vessel and impossible for a slow one, so this cannot be a constant."));
c.push(H2("9.5  Position sources"));
c.push(P("A ROC position arrives three ways, all funnelling into one feed path: manually from the card, pushed by any external process to the API, or read from a real NMEA-0183 GPS stream over TCP or UDP. Sentences are checksum-verified and a void fix is rejected — a GPS feed is untrusted input. `gps_sim.py` is a faithful stand-in emitter for use when no hardware is present, and the console can spawn one per ROC automatically."));

// 10 ------------------------------------------------------------------------
c.push(H1("10  HTTP API reference"));
c.push(P("JSON in, JSON out. Command endpoints are refused unless the console is armed, and every refusal names its reason."));
c.push(P("EVERY POST MUST BE LABELED `Content-Type: application/json` (a charset is fine), or it is refused 415 and logged. A web page on another site can make a browser send a form, multipart or text/plain POST without a preflight; a JSON label forces the browser to ask first, and the console answers no OPTIONS request and sends no Access-Control header, so that POST is never sent. The exceptions are the stops: `/api/cmd/stop`, `/api/cmd/pause`, and `/api/cmd/estop` when it latches are honored whatever they are labeled. An E-STOP body that cannot be read, is not an object, or does not say off LATCHES; only `on: false` or `on: 0` releases, and a release must be labeled JSON (`post_refusal`, `estop_wants_latch`)."));
c.push(TBL(["Endpoint", "Method", "Purpose"], [
  ["`/api/state`", "GET", "Current state snapshot"],
  ["`/events`", "GET (SSE)", "Live state stream"],
  ["`/api/vessel`", "GET / POST", "Active vessel configuration; POST switches vessel (gated disarmed + idle)"],
  ["`/api/vessels`", "GET", "Available vessel configurations"],
  ["`/api/connect`, `/api/disconnect`", "POST", "Attach or detach the VCU link"],
  ["`/api/cmd/arm`, `/api/cmd/estop`", "POST", "Arm gate and command e-stop"],
  ["`/api/cmd/upload`, `/api/cmd/start`, `/api/cmd/pause`, `/api/cmd/stop`", "POST", "Run control"],
  ["`/api/cmd/goto`, `/api/cmd/rth`, `/api/cmd/transit`, `/api/cmd/hold`", "POST", "Behaviors"],
  ["`/api/cmd/sethome`, `/api/cmd/spawn`, `/api/cmd/reset`, `/api/cmd/energy`", "POST", "Home, simulator placement, power-cycle, energy"],
  ["`/api/roc`", "GET / POST", "ROC registry, lifecycle, GPS attachment, HOME selection, position push"],
  ["`/api/mission`", "GET / POST", "Persisted mission plan, with its revision `rev`. A POST that is not a plan is a 400 and one made to an older revision a 409 carrying the revision on disk; neither writes."],
  ["`/api/enc`, `/api/chartinfo`", "GET", "Chart features and the cell / confidence under the vessel"],
  ["`/api/waterlevel`, `/api/tide`, `/api/env`", "GET / POST", "Water level, tide, environment"],
  ["`/api/ais`", "GET", "Nearby traffic, proxied from the AIS service"],
  ["`/api/logs`, `/api/logevent`", "GET / POST", "Session recordings and event injection"],
], [3400, 1500, 4460]));
c.push(SP());
c.push(P("`/api/roc` takes an `op` field: `add`, `update`, `offset`, `motion`, `confirm`, `hold`, `gps_attach`, `gps_detach`, `remove`, `select_home`, `clear_home`, `feed`. Every op returns the fresh snapshot so a client stays in sync without a second request. `feed` is the external position push — any GPS bridge or ship navigation system can post to it."));

// 11 ------------------------------------------------------------------------
c.push(H1("11  Data formats and persistence"));
c.push(TBL(["File", "Format", "Contents"], [
  ["`mission.json`", "JSON", "The committed plan: waypoints, survey lines, speed, arrival radius, completion mode, buffer"],
  ["`roc_config.json`", "JSON", "ROC definitions, placements and lifecycle state — capped at the 3 most recent (ROC_PERSIST_MAX), HOME always retained; enforced on save AND load, so an oversized file heals itself"],
  ["`vessels/<id>.json`", "JSON", "One vessel's complete configuration (chapter 4)"],
  ["`logs/*.jsonl`", "JSONL", "Append-only session recording: every command, setting, state transition and telemetry sample"],
  ["`charts/`", "Tiles + JSON", "Chart tile and ENC feature caches, regenerated at runtime"],
], [2300, 1400, 5660]));
c.push(SP());
c.push(P("Recordings are the input to the playback view, which replays a session read-only on the same chart: the vessel drives its recorded track, plans and commanded routes appear as they were sent, and a timeline lists every command and state transition including the refused ones."));
c.push(P("NOTHING IN THE CONSOLE REMOVES A RECORDING OR A CACHED CHART. The recordings are the operator's records and the cache is what lets an area already seen plan with the network down, so thinning either is the operator's call. `StorageWatch` - started by `main()`, never at import - measures instead, at start and every `STORAGE_CHECK_S`: what `logs/` and `charts/` hold and what they occupy, priced by the allocation unit of the drive each folder's real path is on (a folder that is a link or junction is measured where it leads; a link inside the tree is not walked); the part of `logs/` older than `STORAGE_OLD_DAYS`; and the free space of each drive the plan, the session log and the chart cache are written to, grouped by device and named by where the files are. The tightest of those drives is published as `storage` in the state. Under `STORAGE_LOW_MB` it is low: the report goes to stderr, the session log records `storage_low` (and `storage_ok` on recovery), and the page raises DISK SPACE LOW once and keeps a DISK LOW pill on the top bar while it lasts - the banner slot is shared, and in the live check the chart's own banner took it four seconds later. When there is room again the pill comes down, and so does the banner if it is still its own."));

// 12 ------------------------------------------------------------------------
c.push(H1("12  Configuration and constants"));
c.push(P("Vessel-specific values are NOT listed here — they live in the vessel file (chapter 4). What follows is console-level."));
c.push(TBL(["Constant", "Default", "Meaning"], [
  ["Web port", "8791", "Console UI"],
  ["`NOGO_RADIUS_M`", "5000 m", "Operating-area half-extent; re-extract after 0.6 × travel"],
  ["`NOGO_MIN_DEPTH_M`", "vessel-derived", "Draft + under-keel clearance; water shallower is nogo"],
  ["`NOGO_BUFFER_M`", "vessel-derived", "Shared keep-clear buffer, operator-adjustable"],
  ["`AIS_SHOW_RADIUS_KM`", "50 km", "DISPLAY filter only — a true range circle from the vessel, clamped to the collect radius. The card reads and sets it in NAUTICAL MILES; the wire and the command-line flags stay metric"],
  ["`AIS_COLLECT_RADIUS_KM`", "150 km", "What the upstream subscription actually covers. Collect WIDE, filter NARROW — the display radius moves freely inside this and never re-subscribes"],
  ["AIS sources", "`auto`", "`--ais-source`, passed to the service's `--source`: any comma list of aisstream / digitraffic / aishub / nmea / opencpn. ALL enabled sources merge into ONE MMSI-keyed registry — per-vessel `srcs` records every reporting feed, `src` names the feed whose position is displayed, and a polled report older than the held position is dropped (static fields still merge). `--ais-nmea udp:PORT|tcp:HOST:PORT` (repeatable) adds local AIVDM endpoints, each with its own named health; `--ais-opencpn [HOST:]PORT` reads OpenCPN's TCP relay; aishub polls at its 1/min limit and needs `$AISHUB_USER` (membership = contributing a feed). Naming an endpoint enables its source"],
  ["Telemetry rate", "4 Hz", "Engine tick and SSE push"],
  ["Telemetry stale after", "2 s", "No frame while the console says it streams: the page says TELEMETRY STALE (6.3)"],
  ["`WATER_STALE_S`", "25 min", "A water level older than this is shown but not applied to charted depths (7.2)"],
  ["Current recomputed", "60 s", "`CurrentsMonitor.SAMPLE_S`; the cycle itself is looked for every 15 min (5.3)"],
  ["Fault clears after", "8 clean ticks / frames", "Two seconds without a failure, on the console's loop and on the page alike (5.1, 6.3)"],
  ["`BUILD_RECHECK_S`", "5 s", "How often the console looks at its own files for a new version on disk (6.4)"],
  ["`STORAGE_LOW_MB`", "2048 MB", "Less free than this on a drive the plan, the session log or the chart cache is written to raises DISK SPACE LOW (11). Nothing is removed automatically"],
  ["`STORAGE_OLD_DAYS` / `STORAGE_CHECK_S`", "30 days / 30 min", "The part of `logs/` older than this is counted apart in the storage readout, which is measured this often (11)"],
  ["Turn arc sampling", "~3 m", "Waypoint spacing along a generated survey turn"],
  ["ROC link thresholds", "5 s / 15 s", "Fresh → stale → lost for a live-fed ROC"],
  ["Distance display unit", "km", "The DIST pill: LONG distances read km or nm, operator's stored choice, applied at the display edge by one formatter (`fmtDist`). Canonical values are meters everywhere; short distances, depths and the LINES table stay metric, chart tiles stay in feet, the AIS card stays in nm"],
], [2600, 1900, 4860]));

// 13 ------------------------------------------------------------------------
c.push(H1("13  Development practice and verification"));
c.push(H2("13.1  Regression harnesses"));
c.push(P("The regression suites guard behavior that has bitten repeatedly. None needs a third-party dependency; several stand up a REAL console and drive it over the API, because accepting a command proves nothing about whether it reached the vessel. The pre-commit hook runs every one of them unless everything staged is Markdown or git metadata, stops any that hangs after SUITE_LIMIT_S, and names the code to look at for each that fails."));
c.push(P("THE LIST BELOW IS READ FROM `tests/` AT BUILD TIME. It used to be a hand-written table introduced by the word “Fourteen”, and by the time anyone counted there were twenty. The descriptions are still written by hand — “what this guards” is prose no parser can invent — but the list and the count come from the filesystem, so a suite added tomorrow appears here without anyone remembering to add it."));
c.push(TBL(["Harness", "Guards"], harnessRows(), [2700, 6660]));
c.push(SP());
c.push(P("Enable the hook once per clone with `git config core.hooksPath .githooks`."));
c.push(H2("13.2  How these harnesses work, and how they break"));
c.push(P("The browser-side harnesses EXTRACT the real functions out of `static/asv.html` by brace matching and run them in Node against synthetic worlds. This tests the shipping code rather than a copy — but it also means the harness ENUMERATES the functions it extracts, so it breaks silently when the code under test gains a new dependency. Treat “harness crashed” exactly as loudly as “check failed”: a suite that cannot run protects nothing, and a suite that prints success while testing dead code is worse than none at all."));
c.push(H2("13.3  Tests with teeth"));
c.push(P("A check that cannot fail is not a check. Every harness in this repository has been verified by MUTATION: deliberately breaking the behavior under test and confirming the specific assertions fail. Where a test asserts that something is refused, it is paired with a case proving the same input is ACCEPTED under valid conditions — otherwise a function that refused everything would pass."));
c.push(H2("13.4  What static checks cannot catch"));
c.push(P("A clearance check on a PLANNED path does not catch FOLLOW OVERSHOOT. An idealised arc can validate perfectly clear and still be tighter than the vessel can hold, in which case the vessel overshoots outboard — into the very keep-out the arc was hugging. Turn geometry must therefore be verified with a live simulation run sampling vessel-versus-nogo at zero buffer, not with geometry alone. This is why the minimum turn radius exists."));

// 14 ------------------------------------------------------------------------
c.push(H1("14  Known limitations and roadmap"));
c.push(B("`RealVcu` command and telemetry codecs are unimplemented by design. The console is a simulator; the real link refuses honestly rather than pretending."));
c.push(B("The simulator caps yaw RATE, not radius, so the modeled minimum turn radius grows linearly with speed. Real hulls hold a roughly constant minimum radius. The cap is calibrated at survey speed, which is where surveys are planned, and is pessimistic at high speed."));
c.push(B("Raw API command and upload calls with no supplied route BYPASS chart-aware routing, because routing is client-side. Rehearse through the interface, or place raw waypoints in open water."));
c.push(B("Chart scale and currency vary. The console is a planning AID and says so in a banner; it is not hydrographically certified, and the operator remains responsible for the plan."));
c.push(B("Traffic data comes from a crowd-sourced network with real coverage gaps. A quiet area is reported as “connected, no vessels reporting” rather than as an error, because those are different conditions."));

// 15 ------------------------------------------------------------------------
c.push(H1("15  Extension guide"));
c.push(H2("15.1  Adding a vessel"));
c.push(P("Write `vessels/<id>.json`. No code changes. See chapter 4."));
c.push(H2("15.2  Adding a behavior"));
c.push(P("Add the command endpoint to the server, a method on `Engine` that builds a route and calls the shared run-route path, and a client function that plans the route against the nogo model before uploading it. Reuse the existing route search and channel lane rather than adding a parallel mechanism — a behavior that routes its own way will diverge from every other behavior the first time the router improves."));
c.push(H2("15.3  Adding a vessel-dependent value"));
c.push(P("Put the field in the vessel schema with a derived fallback so older vessel files still load, read it in `apply_vessel()` so a live switch re-derives it, mirror it to the client through `/api/vessel` if the browser needs it, and add a regression assertion that the value actually CHANGES between two different vessels. That last step is what catches a value that was wired up but never re-derived."));
c.push(H2("15.4  Adding UI"));
c.push(P("Give every interactive element a stable unique id (the multi-monitor bridge forwards by id), key handlers off class or `data-*`, register any new pop-out card in the bridged-selector list and the card-title map, and rebuild card rows only when the underlying SET changes — rebuilding on every telemetry frame will clobber a field the operator is typing into."));
c.push(H2("15.5  House rules"));
c.push(B("No hardcoded vessel constants. Ever. The vessel file is the single source of truth."));
c.push(B("Keep the console core vendor-neutral. Vessel files may name real vessels; the console may not."));
c.push(B("Never weaken the safety model: arm-gating, e-stop, link-loss failsafe and the refusal-with-a-reason contract."));
c.push(B("Update the relevant README in the same change as the behavior it documents, and rebuild this manual from its script."));

// ---- document --------------------------------------------------------------
write("asv-simulator-technical-manual.docx", c);
